INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$d3104a68$SATQ$, $SATQ$507b9440-f15f-4cb8-968b-fb7b37fc0f74$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Birds sing to communicate over potentially great distances. For this reason, many researchers believe that birds in densely vegetated habitats generally sing at lower frequencies than birds living in comparatively sparse habitats, since dense vegetation tends to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"db7ff216-c990-4257-92df-c847baa1eb49","content":"<p>exceed</p>"},{"id":"71a1d93f-89d9-4ee2-82ad-2f3696dfd82a","content":"<p>diminish</p>"},{"id":"47e48d16-b1d4-48c2-89db-f0c2822d81e9","content":"<p>encompass</p>"},{"id":"f3a0c1fb-654a-4ab3-848d-9ad95c2a5784","content":"<p>conceal</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["71a1d93f-89d9-4ee2-82ad-2f3696dfd82a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of birdsong. In this context, "diminish" means reduce or lessen. The text establishes that birds sing to communicate over potentially large distances and that researchers believe that birds that live in densely vegetated habitats sing at lower frequencies than birds that live in comparatively sparsely vegetated habitats. The text then explains that researchers hold this belief due to some effect of dense vegetation on the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel. If birds sing to communicate over long distances, it would be logical to conclude that researchers believe that birds in densely vegetated habitats sing at lower frequencies than birds in relatively sparse habitats do because dense vegetation diminishes the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that dense vegetation can "exceed," or surpass or go beyond, the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel. The text suggests that dense vegetation reduces the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel, not that dense vegetation goes farther than that distance. Choice C is incorrect because it would not be sensible to say that dense vegetation can "encompass," or enclose or encircle, the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel. The distance that sounds can travel is not an object that can be enclosed or encircled by vegetation. Choice D is incorrect because there is no information in the text indicating that dense vegetation can "conceal," or hide or keep from being observed, the distance that high-frequency sounds travel. Instead, the text suggests that dense vegetation reduces the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d3104a68","external_id":"507b9440-f15f-4cb8-968b-fb7b37fc0f74","vaultid":null,"uId":"eaa19323-713f-4022-a5d9-4fe33bed7ee0","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Birds sing to communicate over potentially great distances. For this reason, many researchers believe that birds in densely vegetated habitats generally sing at lower frequencies than birds living in comparatively sparse habitats, since dense vegetation tends to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"db7ff216-c990-4257-92df-c847baa1eb49","content":"<p>exceed</p>"},{"id":"71a1d93f-89d9-4ee2-82ad-2f3696dfd82a","content":"<p>diminish</p>"},{"id":"47e48d16-b1d4-48c2-89db-f0c2822d81e9","content":"<p>encompass</p>"},{"id":"f3a0c1fb-654a-4ab3-848d-9ad95c2a5784","content":"<p>conceal</p>"}],"keys":["71a1d93f-89d9-4ee2-82ad-2f3696dfd82a"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of birdsong. In this context, \"diminish\" means reduce or lessen. The text establishes that birds sing to communicate over potentially large distances and that researchers believe that birds that live in densely vegetated habitats sing at lower frequencies than birds that live in comparatively sparsely vegetated habitats. The text then explains that researchers hold this belief due to some effect of dense vegetation on the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel. If birds sing to communicate over long distances, it would be logical to conclude that researchers believe that birds in densely vegetated habitats sing at lower frequencies than birds in relatively sparse habitats do because dense vegetation diminishes the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that dense vegetation can \"exceed,\" or surpass or go beyond, the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel. The text suggests that dense vegetation reduces the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel, not that dense vegetation goes farther than that distance. Choice C is incorrect because it would not be sensible to say that dense vegetation can \"encompass,\" or enclose or encircle, the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel. The distance that sounds can travel is not an object that can be enclosed or encircled by vegetation. Choice D is incorrect because there is no information in the text indicating that dense vegetation can \"conceal,\" or hide or keep from being observed, the distance that high-frequency sounds travel. Instead, the text suggests that dense vegetation reduces the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323040,"updateDate":1730147323040}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323040, 1730147323040),
    ($SATQ$d3ca5d59$SATQ$, $SATQ$d7b12c3e-7c14-42b7-b6e3-968401ad557d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$93ee2dae-c0fd-48d0-96cb-5003e3e0373d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Stephen Hannock&rsquo;s luminous landscape paintings are appealing to viewers but have elicited little commentary from contemporary critics, a phenomenon that may be due to the very fact that the paintings seem so <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>. Many critics focus their attention on art that is cryptic or overtly challenging.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4da0ccb7-4620-4b16-95ab-2149b9c05529","content":"<p>innovative</p>"},{"id":"f209e2db-0713-44be-aad9-89f91dfba71c","content":"<p>subversive</p>"},{"id":"75b056f2-cc76-47c0-b683-22104fa96e34","content":"<p>profound</p>"},{"id":"de6a13cc-1edb-4e99-9125-c926f439c1a4","content":"<p>accessible</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["de6a13cc-1edb-4e99-9125-c926f439c1a4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. When talking about a thing, &ldquo;accessible&rdquo; means &ldquo;easy to understand.&rdquo; This sets up the contrast in the next sentence, which tells us that critics mostly focus on art that is &ldquo;cryptic or challenging&rdquo; (meaning not easy to understand). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. The next sentence tells us that critics focus on art that is mysterious and challenging. If Hannock&rsquo;s paintings are &ldquo;innovative&rdquo; (meaning advanced and original), then critics probably would comment on them. Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. The next sentence tells us that critics focus on art that is mysterious and challenging. If Hannock&rsquo;s paintings are &ldquo;subversive&rdquo; (meaning disruptive and revolutionary), then critics probably would comment on them. Choice C is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. The next sentence tells us that critics focus on art that is mysterious and challenging. If Hannock&rsquo;s paintings are &ldquo;profound&rdquo; (meaning very deep and insightful), then critics probably would comment on them. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-H002$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$9d652343-78fa-40c0-a5ac-e7b34df1ef4f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d3ca5d59","external_id":"d7b12c3e-7c14-42b7-b6e3-968401ad557d","vaultid":"93ee2dae-c0fd-48d0-96cb-5003e3e0373d","uId":"7d00c1b1-349e-49fb-a596-b7956d9e2891","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Stephen Hannock&rsquo;s luminous landscape paintings are appealing to viewers but have elicited little commentary from contemporary critics, a phenomenon that may be due to the very fact that the paintings seem so <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>. Many critics focus their attention on art that is cryptic or overtly challenging.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4da0ccb7-4620-4b16-95ab-2149b9c05529","content":"<p>innovative</p>"},{"id":"f209e2db-0713-44be-aad9-89f91dfba71c","content":"<p>subversive</p>"},{"id":"75b056f2-cc76-47c0-b683-22104fa96e34","content":"<p>profound</p>"},{"id":"de6a13cc-1edb-4e99-9125-c926f439c1a4","content":"<p>accessible</p>"}],"keys":["de6a13cc-1edb-4e99-9125-c926f439c1a4"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. When talking about a thing, &ldquo;accessible&rdquo; means &ldquo;easy to understand.&rdquo; This sets up the contrast in the next sentence, which tells us that critics mostly focus on art that is &ldquo;cryptic or challenging&rdquo; (meaning not easy to understand). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. The next sentence tells us that critics focus on art that is mysterious and challenging. If Hannock&rsquo;s paintings are &ldquo;innovative&rdquo; (meaning advanced and original), then critics probably would comment on them. Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. The next sentence tells us that critics focus on art that is mysterious and challenging. If Hannock&rsquo;s paintings are &ldquo;subversive&rdquo; (meaning disruptive and revolutionary), then critics probably would comment on them. Choice C is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. The next sentence tells us that critics focus on art that is mysterious and challenging. If Hannock&rsquo;s paintings are &ldquo;profound&rdquo; (meaning very deep and insightful), then critics probably would comment on them. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-H002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9d652343-78fa-40c0-a5ac-e7b34df1ef4f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$d4732483$SATQ$, $SATQ$19cb3d61-e50c-4c69-8608-9f289cd7839d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$73927402-4f84-428f-b252-102dc8c19069$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Studying late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century artifacts from an agricultural and domestic site in Texas, archaeologist Ayana O. Flewellen found that Black women employed as farm workers utilized hook-and-eye closures to fasten their clothes at the waist, giving themselves a silhouette similar to the one that was popular in contemporary fashion and typically achieved through more restrictive garments such as corsets. Flewellen argues that this sartorial practice shows that these women balanced hegemonic ideals of femininity with the requirements of their physically demanding occupation.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d01c8978-4d3a-4fa5-9d9f-b5019f1737a4","content":"<p>To describe an unexpected discovery that altered a researcher&rsquo;s view of how rapidly fashions among Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas changed during the period</p>"},{"id":"bba1744c-4602-4c0a-b5f9-9bca8046ac61","content":"<p>To discuss research that investigated the ways in which Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas used fashion practices to resist traditional gender ideals</p>"},{"id":"a0a61b0b-3cd3-4d0c-bd2d-3c6add71f349","content":"<p>To evaluate a scholarly work that offers explanations for the impact of urban fashion ideals on Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas</p>"},{"id":"9ff833ba-b76e-4061-b18e-7c0aed985b4d","content":"<p>To summarize the findings of a study that explored factors influencing a fashion practice among Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9ff833ba-b76e-4061-b18e-7c0aed985b4d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The text provides an overview of a scholarly work discussing the fashion practices of Black female farmworkers in late 19th- and early 20th-century Texas, and how these practices were influenced by both the fashion ideals of the time and the demands of farmwork.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text never discusses the rate of fashion change among Black female farmworkers. The text also never categorizes Flewellen&rsquo;s findings as "unexpected." Choice B is incorrect. The text actually explains that Black female farmworkers were trying to achieve traditional feminine ideals, not resist them. Choice C is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t evaluate a scholarly work but rather simply describes it. Furthermore, the text is focused on "agricultural and domestic" fashion, not urban fashion as this choice suggests.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-H009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$643044f9-e6fa-4908-9ae0-6372c509f32d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d4732483","external_id":"19cb3d61-e50c-4c69-8608-9f289cd7839d","vaultid":"73927402-4f84-428f-b252-102dc8c19069","uId":"0a8ab139-6641-4c5d-b8bf-5cd25d41c750","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Studying late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century artifacts from an agricultural and domestic site in Texas, archaeologist Ayana O. Flewellen found that Black women employed as farm workers utilized hook-and-eye closures to fasten their clothes at the waist, giving themselves a silhouette similar to the one that was popular in contemporary fashion and typically achieved through more restrictive garments such as corsets. Flewellen argues that this sartorial practice shows that these women balanced hegemonic ideals of femininity with the requirements of their physically demanding occupation.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d01c8978-4d3a-4fa5-9d9f-b5019f1737a4","content":"<p>To describe an unexpected discovery that altered a researcher&rsquo;s view of how rapidly fashions among Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas changed during the period</p>"},{"id":"bba1744c-4602-4c0a-b5f9-9bca8046ac61","content":"<p>To discuss research that investigated the ways in which Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas used fashion practices to resist traditional gender ideals</p>"},{"id":"a0a61b0b-3cd3-4d0c-bd2d-3c6add71f349","content":"<p>To evaluate a scholarly work that offers explanations for the impact of urban fashion ideals on Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas</p>"},{"id":"9ff833ba-b76e-4061-b18e-7c0aed985b4d","content":"<p>To summarize the findings of a study that explored factors influencing a fashion practice among Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas</p>"}],"keys":["9ff833ba-b76e-4061-b18e-7c0aed985b4d"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The text provides an overview of a scholarly work discussing the fashion practices of Black female farmworkers in late 19th- and early 20th-century Texas, and how these practices were influenced by both the fashion ideals of the time and the demands of farmwork.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text never discusses the rate of fashion change among Black female farmworkers. The text also never categorizes Flewellen&rsquo;s findings as \"unexpected.\" Choice B is incorrect. The text actually explains that Black female farmworkers were trying to achieve traditional feminine ideals, not resist them. Choice C is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t evaluate a scholarly work but rather simply describes it. Furthermore, the text is focused on \"agricultural and domestic\" fashion, not urban fashion as this choice suggests.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-H009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"643044f9-e6fa-4908-9ae0-6372c509f32d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959375,"updateDate":1691007959375}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959375, 1691007959375),
    ($SATQ$d4a8f7cb$SATQ$, $SATQ$5f3280cd-e6b6-460d-9cd2-eff1f3095600$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ac639fe1-8bb0-40c6-9f03-7a6e7bb4832c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Dance choreographer Jawole Willa Jo Zollar aims to give people the opportunity to be <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> her creative process. For example, live performances of her dance <em>HairStories</em>, which debuted in 2001, featured videos of people across the United States talking about their hair and audience members sharing pictures of their interesting hairstyles.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6bcc8115-3107-4eff-ba9d-e4a9bb9d3d71","content":"<p>nervous about</p>"},{"id":"083a0171-a235-4f80-9ec5-813aa108f0da","content":"<p>completed by</p>"},{"id":"c5042bdd-43a5-4dc1-97fb-cf6f3a096dc6","content":"<p>delayed by</p>"},{"id":"595d80ad-b4c0-4c17-ba1f-8dda500b664c","content":"<p>involved in</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["595d80ad-b4c0-4c17-ba1f-8dda500b664c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Involved in&rdquo; means &ldquo;playing an active role in.&rdquo; This fits the context clues describing how Zollar includes the audience in her shows by incorporating their stories and pictures. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. &ldquo;Nervous&rdquo; means &ldquo;worried or anxious about.&rdquo; Nothing in the text suggests that people would be nervous about Zollar&rsquo;s creative process. Choice B is incorrect. &ldquo;Completed by&rdquo; means &ldquo;made whole by.&rdquo; It doesn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Zollar&rsquo;s shows would make her audience complete. Choice C is incorrect. &ldquo;Delayed by&rdquo; means &ldquo;made late by.&rdquo; Nothing in the text suggests that people would be delayed by Zollar&rsquo;s creative process. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-E006$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3671cc0e-9cf7-4f6a-bcb4-97f01fe17cba$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d4a8f7cb","external_id":"5f3280cd-e6b6-460d-9cd2-eff1f3095600","vaultid":"ac639fe1-8bb0-40c6-9f03-7a6e7bb4832c","uId":"0a420600-84fd-455f-a03b-ac5b47e65efd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Dance choreographer Jawole Willa Jo Zollar aims to give people the opportunity to be <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> her creative process. For example, live performances of her dance <em>HairStories</em>, which debuted in 2001, featured videos of people across the United States talking about their hair and audience members sharing pictures of their interesting hairstyles.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6bcc8115-3107-4eff-ba9d-e4a9bb9d3d71","content":"<p>nervous about</p>"},{"id":"083a0171-a235-4f80-9ec5-813aa108f0da","content":"<p>completed by</p>"},{"id":"c5042bdd-43a5-4dc1-97fb-cf6f3a096dc6","content":"<p>delayed by</p>"},{"id":"595d80ad-b4c0-4c17-ba1f-8dda500b664c","content":"<p>involved in</p>"}],"keys":["595d80ad-b4c0-4c17-ba1f-8dda500b664c"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Involved in&rdquo; means &ldquo;playing an active role in.&rdquo; This fits the context clues describing how Zollar includes the audience in her shows by incorporating their stories and pictures. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. &ldquo;Nervous&rdquo; means &ldquo;worried or anxious about.&rdquo; Nothing in the text suggests that people would be nervous about Zollar&rsquo;s creative process. Choice B is incorrect. &ldquo;Completed by&rdquo; means &ldquo;made whole by.&rdquo; It doesn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Zollar&rsquo;s shows would make her audience complete. Choice C is incorrect. &ldquo;Delayed by&rdquo; means &ldquo;made late by.&rdquo; Nothing in the text suggests that people would be delayed by Zollar&rsquo;s creative process. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-E006","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3671cc0e-9cf7-4f6a-bcb4-97f01fe17cba","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$d4e8662d$SATQ$, $SATQ$a20b2ca0-69db-4750-ae47-4d37bd2c93c7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The percentage of US forest land that a 2023 federal report identified as being either mature or old growth exceeds other recent estimates. Given how little <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> there is among scientists regarding the scope of these categories, this discrepancy shouldn&rsquo;t be surprising: forest researchers regularly dispute one another&rsquo;s classifications.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f5185f26-7606-4190-b918-829ed68c2278","content":"<p>deliberation</p>"},{"id":"7b3f5242-fedf-4f8f-9e36-8769ac4f8c9f","content":"<p>vigilance</p>"},{"id":"dfc6acd7-02fb-4802-8e2c-e4e943a9705d","content":"<p>interest</p>"},{"id":"08ae2d9c-7091-44ad-8474-f7954883bfed","content":"<p>consensus</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["08ae2d9c-7091-44ad-8474-f7954883bfed"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of US forest land classifications. In this context, "consensus" means agreement. The text indicates that a percentage given in a US forest land report differs from other estimates and that this discrepancy isn&rsquo;t surprising, given that forest researchers regularly dispute one another&rsquo;s classifications of parts of US forest land as either mature or old growth. This context conveys that there is little agreement when it comes to these categories.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text states that there is often dispute among forest researchers about classifications of US forest land as either mature or old growth, which directly indicates that when it comes to these categories, there is much "deliberation," or discussion and consideration, not little deliberation. Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that there is often dispute among forest researchers about classifications of US forest land as either mature or old growth; this doesn&rsquo;t imply that there is little "vigilance," or careful attention, among researchers when it comes to these categories but rather conveys that researchers do pay attention to forest land classifications and question one another&rsquo;s decisions. Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that there is often dispute among forest researchers about classifications of US forest land as either mature or old growth; this doesn&rsquo;t imply that there is little "interest," or concern, among researchers when it comes to these categories but rather conveys that researchers are concerned with forest land classifications and pay attention to one another&rsquo;s classifications.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d4e8662d","external_id":"a20b2ca0-69db-4750-ae47-4d37bd2c93c7","vaultid":null,"uId":"a2f1c20a-8b2d-42d9-b6f4-d86625719acd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The percentage of US forest land that a 2023 federal report identified as being either mature or old growth exceeds other recent estimates. Given how little <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> there is among scientists regarding the scope of these categories, this discrepancy shouldn&rsquo;t be surprising: forest researchers regularly dispute one another&rsquo;s classifications.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f5185f26-7606-4190-b918-829ed68c2278","content":"<p>deliberation</p>"},{"id":"7b3f5242-fedf-4f8f-9e36-8769ac4f8c9f","content":"<p>vigilance</p>"},{"id":"dfc6acd7-02fb-4802-8e2c-e4e943a9705d","content":"<p>interest</p>"},{"id":"08ae2d9c-7091-44ad-8474-f7954883bfed","content":"<p>consensus</p>"}],"keys":["08ae2d9c-7091-44ad-8474-f7954883bfed"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of US forest land classifications. In this context, \"consensus\" means agreement. The text indicates that a percentage given in a US forest land report differs from other estimates and that this discrepancy isn&rsquo;t surprising, given that forest researchers regularly dispute one another&rsquo;s classifications of parts of US forest land as either mature or old growth. This context conveys that there is little agreement when it comes to these categories.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text states that there is often dispute among forest researchers about classifications of US forest land as either mature or old growth, which directly indicates that when it comes to these categories, there is much \"deliberation,\" or discussion and consideration, not little deliberation. Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that there is often dispute among forest researchers about classifications of US forest land as either mature or old growth; this doesn&rsquo;t imply that there is little \"vigilance,\" or careful attention, among researchers when it comes to these categories but rather conveys that researchers do pay attention to forest land classifications and question one another&rsquo;s decisions. Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that there is often dispute among forest researchers about classifications of US forest land as either mature or old growth; this doesn&rsquo;t imply that there is little \"interest,\" or concern, among researchers when it comes to these categories but rather conveys that researchers are concerned with forest land classifications and pay attention to one another&rsquo;s classifications.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323041,"updateDate":1730147323041}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323041, 1730147323041),
    ($SATQ$d5235d39$SATQ$, $SATQ$902d1814-a730-4637-b69f-7bb1b8b9f400$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ac9b0046-cd9a-4b2d-b5cb-9cad5328a056$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>The Mule Bone</em>, a 1930 play written by Zora Neale Hurston and Langston Hughes, is perhaps the best-known of the few examples of <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in literature. Most writers prefer working alone, and given that working together cost Hurston and Hughes their friendship, it is not hard to see why.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6da0afd3-a64a-41c5-96ff-438159b83175","content":"<p>characterization</p>"},{"id":"e61b4a71-3437-43d0-a0f8-9b259e1a2643","content":"<p>interpretation</p>"},{"id":"affc36f2-9723-48a1-89be-c00fe1124ece","content":"<p>collaboration</p>"},{"id":"0d40d9e4-390e-4f53-9055-73347ee03aa8","content":"<p>commercialization</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["affc36f2-9723-48a1-89be-c00fe1124ece"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it logically and precisely completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of <em>The Mule Bone</em>, a play that Zora Neale Hurston and Langston Hughes wrote together. In this context, &ldquo;collaboration&rdquo; means working together with someone to write a literary work. The text indicates that most writers prefer to work alone and that working together destroyed the friendship between Hurston and Hughes. This establishes that <em>The Mule Bone </em>is a relatively rare example of collaboration in literature. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect&nbsp;because in this context, &ldquo;characterization&rdquo; would mean a literary work&rsquo;s portrayal of characters&rsquo; psychological experiences and motivations, but the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss characterization in <em>The Mule Bone </em>specifically or in collaborative works more generally. Choice B is incorrect&nbsp;because in this context, &ldquo;interpretation&rdquo; would mean the explanation of a literary work&rsquo;s meaning or significance, but the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss how readers or critics have interpreted <em>The Mule Bone</em>; instead, the text discusses how the play was written collaboratively and how the writing process affected the two authors. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;commercialization&rdquo; would mean writing a literary work in such a way as to ensure its commercial appeal, but the text never discusses commercial appeal as a factor in the writing of <em>The Mule Bone </em>specifically or the writing of collaborative works more generally. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-908-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$95c27926-7391-4957-abc5-4f8f83624ae2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d5235d39","external_id":"902d1814-a730-4637-b69f-7bb1b8b9f400","vaultid":"ac9b0046-cd9a-4b2d-b5cb-9cad5328a056","uId":"ca80646a-383c-49e4-923a-ed7dde0a9320","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>The Mule Bone</em>, a 1930 play written by Zora Neale Hurston and Langston Hughes, is perhaps the best-known of the few examples of <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in literature. Most writers prefer working alone, and given that working together cost Hurston and Hughes their friendship, it is not hard to see why.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6da0afd3-a64a-41c5-96ff-438159b83175","content":"<p>characterization</p>"},{"id":"e61b4a71-3437-43d0-a0f8-9b259e1a2643","content":"<p>interpretation</p>"},{"id":"affc36f2-9723-48a1-89be-c00fe1124ece","content":"<p>collaboration</p>"},{"id":"0d40d9e4-390e-4f53-9055-73347ee03aa8","content":"<p>commercialization</p>"}],"keys":["affc36f2-9723-48a1-89be-c00fe1124ece"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it logically and precisely completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of <em>The Mule Bone</em>, a play that Zora Neale Hurston and Langston Hughes wrote together. In this context, &ldquo;collaboration&rdquo; means working together with someone to write a literary work. The text indicates that most writers prefer to work alone and that working together destroyed the friendship between Hurston and Hughes. This establishes that <em>The Mule Bone </em>is a relatively rare example of collaboration in literature. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect&nbsp;because in this context, &ldquo;characterization&rdquo; would mean a literary work&rsquo;s portrayal of characters&rsquo; psychological experiences and motivations, but the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss characterization in <em>The Mule Bone </em>specifically or in collaborative works more generally. Choice B is incorrect&nbsp;because in this context, &ldquo;interpretation&rdquo; would mean the explanation of a literary work&rsquo;s meaning or significance, but the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss how readers or critics have interpreted <em>The Mule Bone</em>; instead, the text discusses how the play was written collaboratively and how the writing process affected the two authors. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;commercialization&rdquo; would mean writing a literary work in such a way as to ensure its commercial appeal, but the text never discusses commercial appeal as a factor in the writing of <em>The Mule Bone </em>specifically or the writing of collaborative works more generally. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-908-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"95c27926-7391-4957-abc5-4f8f83624ae2","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959354,"updateDate":1691007959354}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959354, 1691007959354),
    ($SATQ$d529c1ad$SATQ$, $SATQ$1aff86ca-a9d5-4205-8ff4-c9fe42e6a26e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Ezra Pound&rsquo;s poetry can be hard to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>: it is dense, experimental, and so full of references and allusions that many readers have a difficult time even identifying the poems&rsquo; subjects.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7881c6a3-7671-43ee-afc3-1bccc66d419f","content":"<p>comprehend</p>"},{"id":"3ec4385c-2225-4bfd-93b7-5c2a0d6b9a36","content":"<p>dislike</p>"},{"id":"e386a5d8-a24e-4c52-b47b-78b368d316cd","content":"<p>interrupt</p>"},{"id":"3040ebe7-eb44-4f81-93fb-f59626721050","content":"<p>overlook</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7881c6a3-7671-43ee-afc3-1bccc66d419f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Ezra Pound&rsquo;s poetry. In this context, &ldquo;comprehend&rdquo; would mean to understand or grasp the meaning of something. The text describes Pound&rsquo;s poetry as &ldquo;dense&rdquo; and &ldquo;experimental,&rdquo; with many references that readers may not understand. The text also states that many readers may not even be able to identify a poem&rsquo;s subject. This context clearly suggests that readers struggle to understand Pound&rsquo;s poetry as a result of its complexity and obscure references.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. To say that Pound&rsquo;s poetry is &ldquo;hard to dislike&rdquo; would suggest that people generally like it, but the text doesn&rsquo;t focus on how likeable or enjoyable the poems are; rather, it focuses on how challenging many readers find it to understand them. Choice C is incorrect. To say that Pound&rsquo;s poetry is &ldquo;hard to interrupt&rdquo; would mean that it is hard to stop or break into it while it&rsquo;s being read or recited, but the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss any such breaks; instead, the text focuses on the challenge of understanding the content of Pound&rsquo;s poems. Choice D is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;overlook&rdquo; would mean look past or fail to notice, so to say that Pound&rsquo;s poetry is &ldquo;hard to overlook&rdquo; would mean that it is very likely to be noticed. The text focuses on the difficulty people have in understanding Pound&rsquo;s poems when reading them, not how noticeable his poetry is in the first place.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d529c1ad","external_id":"1aff86ca-a9d5-4205-8ff4-c9fe42e6a26e","vaultid":null,"uId":"384a37b1-3a98-4eba-9ad1-da4294a86d23","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Ezra Pound&rsquo;s poetry can be hard to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>: it is dense, experimental, and so full of references and allusions that many readers have a difficult time even identifying the poems&rsquo; subjects.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7881c6a3-7671-43ee-afc3-1bccc66d419f","content":"<p>comprehend</p>"},{"id":"3ec4385c-2225-4bfd-93b7-5c2a0d6b9a36","content":"<p>dislike</p>"},{"id":"e386a5d8-a24e-4c52-b47b-78b368d316cd","content":"<p>interrupt</p>"},{"id":"3040ebe7-eb44-4f81-93fb-f59626721050","content":"<p>overlook</p>"}],"keys":["7881c6a3-7671-43ee-afc3-1bccc66d419f"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Ezra Pound&rsquo;s poetry. In this context, &ldquo;comprehend&rdquo; would mean to understand or grasp the meaning of something. The text describes Pound&rsquo;s poetry as &ldquo;dense&rdquo; and &ldquo;experimental,&rdquo; with many references that readers may not understand. The text also states that many readers may not even be able to identify a poem&rsquo;s subject. This context clearly suggests that readers struggle to understand Pound&rsquo;s poetry as a result of its complexity and obscure references.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. To say that Pound&rsquo;s poetry is &ldquo;hard to dislike&rdquo; would suggest that people generally like it, but the text doesn&rsquo;t focus on how likeable or enjoyable the poems are; rather, it focuses on how challenging many readers find it to understand them. Choice C is incorrect. To say that Pound&rsquo;s poetry is &ldquo;hard to interrupt&rdquo; would mean that it is hard to stop or break into it while it&rsquo;s being read or recited, but the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss any such breaks; instead, the text focuses on the challenge of understanding the content of Pound&rsquo;s poems. Choice D is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;overlook&rdquo; would mean look past or fail to notice, so to say that Pound&rsquo;s poetry is &ldquo;hard to overlook&rdquo; would mean that it is very likely to be noticed. The text focuses on the difficulty people have in understanding Pound&rsquo;s poems when reading them, not how noticeable his poetry is in the first place.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771167,"updateDate":1776362771167}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771167, 1776362771167),
    ($SATQ$d5600e74$SATQ$, $SATQ$7bc9be38-c0b9-4055-8eb5-e338130c51b9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Pam Mu&ntilde;oz Ryan&rsquo;s 2020 novel <em>Ma&ntilde;analand</em>. In the village where Max lives, there is an old fortress called La Reina. Children in the village say that the fortress is haunted.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For as long as he could remember, Max had begged Pap&aacute; [his father] to take him to see La Reina and the ruins up close. He&rsquo;d be a hero among his friends if he was the first boy to cross the haunted gates! Just because Pap&aacute; didn&rsquo;t believe in ghosts didn&rsquo;t mean they weren&rsquo;t there. Maybe this summer Pap&aacute; would finally take him. He <em>was</em> almost twelve.</p>
</blockquote>
<p style="text-align: right;"><span class="dap_copyright">&copy;2020 by Pam Mu&ntilde;oz Ryan</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"16271243-0be3-48d5-a3ed-38183dc73e72","content":"<p>To portray how proud Max&rsquo;s father is of Max</p>"},{"id":"2ea4d038-5d52-4bfe-b273-3b96f3f9a18c","content":"<p>To explain why Max doesn&rsquo;t want to grow up yet</p>"},{"id":"11b07a75-a6f0-4057-9df1-86aed0ad66ab","content":"<p>To criticize Max for disliking summer</p>"},{"id":"402afa18-59c1-4f1d-8d74-483b42d2e81b","content":"<p>To show how much Max wants to visit La Reina</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["402afa18-59c1-4f1d-8d74-483b42d2e81b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall purpose of the text. The text indicates that "for as long as he could remember," Max had "begged" his father to take him to La Reina. This point is later emphasized in the text by indicating that "this summer" his father might "finally take" Max to visit La Reina. Thus, the purpose of the text as a whole is to show how much Max wants to visit La Reina.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text does not discuss Papa&rsquo;s feelings toward Max. Rather, it mentions that Max has long wanted Papa to take him to La Reina and that, unlike Max and some other children, Papa does not believe that La Reina is haunted. Choice B is incorrect. The text mentions that Max is "almost twelve" at the time, which suggests anticipation of growing up rather than refusal to. Choice C is incorrect. The text indicates that Max hopes to visit La Reina during the current summer, but nothing suggests that Max dislikes summer.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d5600e74","external_id":"7bc9be38-c0b9-4055-8eb5-e338130c51b9","vaultid":null,"uId":"ee33fbc4-0490-49f4-8393-c3efc14cea61","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Pam Mu&ntilde;oz Ryan&rsquo;s 2020 novel <em>Ma&ntilde;analand</em>. In the village where Max lives, there is an old fortress called La Reina. Children in the village say that the fortress is haunted.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>For as long as he could remember, Max had begged Pap&aacute; [his father] to take him to see La Reina and the ruins up close. He&rsquo;d be a hero among his friends if he was the first boy to cross the haunted gates! Just because Pap&aacute; didn&rsquo;t believe in ghosts didn&rsquo;t mean they weren&rsquo;t there. Maybe this summer Pap&aacute; would finally take him. He <em>was</em> almost twelve.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><span class=\"dap_copyright\">&copy;2020 by Pam Mu&ntilde;oz Ryan</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"16271243-0be3-48d5-a3ed-38183dc73e72","content":"<p>To portray how proud Max&rsquo;s father is of Max</p>"},{"id":"2ea4d038-5d52-4bfe-b273-3b96f3f9a18c","content":"<p>To explain why Max doesn&rsquo;t want to grow up yet</p>"},{"id":"11b07a75-a6f0-4057-9df1-86aed0ad66ab","content":"<p>To criticize Max for disliking summer</p>"},{"id":"402afa18-59c1-4f1d-8d74-483b42d2e81b","content":"<p>To show how much Max wants to visit La Reina</p>"}],"keys":["402afa18-59c1-4f1d-8d74-483b42d2e81b"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall purpose of the text. The text indicates that \"for as long as he could remember,\" Max had \"begged\" his father to take him to La Reina. This point is later emphasized in the text by indicating that \"this summer\" his father might \"finally take\" Max to visit La Reina. Thus, the purpose of the text as a whole is to show how much Max wants to visit La Reina.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text does not discuss Papa&rsquo;s feelings toward Max. Rather, it mentions that Max has long wanted Papa to take him to La Reina and that, unlike Max and some other children, Papa does not believe that La Reina is haunted. Choice B is incorrect. The text mentions that Max is \"almost twelve\" at the time, which suggests anticipation of growing up rather than refusal to. Choice C is incorrect. The text indicates that Max hopes to visit La Reina during the current summer, but nothing suggests that Max dislikes summer.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669209,"updateDate":1743430669209}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669209, 1743430669209),
    ($SATQ$d563bf65$SATQ$, $SATQ$ae604376-db25-47d7-8ff4-925884ae819a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Jean Webster&rsquo;s 1912 novel <em>Daddy-Long-Legs. </em>The narrator is a young college student writing letters detailing her weekly experiences.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[The college is] organizing the Freshman basket-ball team and there&rsquo;s just a chance that I shall make it. I&rsquo;m little of course, but terribly quick and wiry and tough. While the others are hopping about in the air, I can dodge under their feet and grab the ball.&nbsp;</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"93f056f9-4ac6-401b-902a-6bd1be3e8757","content":"<p>To compare basketball with other sports</p>"},{"id":"a021a661-a217-4cfe-9c05-037b303506d2","content":"<p>To provide details of how to play basketball</p>"},{"id":"d93c5ea2-3891-462a-abfe-d843f6d1a9b5","content":"<p>To state how players will be chosen for the basketball team</p>"},{"id":"388d5b91-a1fb-45d3-822c-249c0d579d00","content":"<p>To explain why the narrator thinks she might make the basketball team</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["388d5b91-a1fb-45d3-822c-249c0d579d00"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. In the first sentence of the text, the narrator states that she thinks there&rsquo;s a chance she will become part of the basketball team at her college. She goes on to explain that she is "quick" and "tough." Based on these characteristics, she thinks she has a chance to join the team. Thus, the main purpose of the text is to explain why the narrator thinks she might make the basketball team.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text focuses solely on basketball and doesn&rsquo;t mention any other kinds of sports. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe aspects of the game of basketball. Instead, it provides the narrator&rsquo;s reasoning for thinking that she might make her school basketball team. Choice C is incorrect. Although the narrator explains why she thinks she will be chosen for the basketball team at her school, the text doesn&rsquo;t go into the general decision-making process or the requirements for being picked for the team.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d563bf65","external_id":"ae604376-db25-47d7-8ff4-925884ae819a","vaultid":null,"uId":"d3a2c8db-64f1-4bb7-a5cd-d5e2e85cff00","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Jean Webster&rsquo;s 1912 novel <em>Daddy-Long-Legs. </em>The narrator is a young college student writing letters detailing her weekly experiences.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[The college is] organizing the Freshman basket-ball team and there&rsquo;s just a chance that I shall make it. I&rsquo;m little of course, but terribly quick and wiry and tough. While the others are hopping about in the air, I can dodge under their feet and grab the ball.&nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"93f056f9-4ac6-401b-902a-6bd1be3e8757","content":"<p>To compare basketball with other sports</p>"},{"id":"a021a661-a217-4cfe-9c05-037b303506d2","content":"<p>To provide details of how to play basketball</p>"},{"id":"d93c5ea2-3891-462a-abfe-d843f6d1a9b5","content":"<p>To state how players will be chosen for the basketball team</p>"},{"id":"388d5b91-a1fb-45d3-822c-249c0d579d00","content":"<p>To explain why the narrator thinks she might make the basketball team</p>"}],"keys":["388d5b91-a1fb-45d3-822c-249c0d579d00"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. In the first sentence of the text, the narrator states that she thinks there&rsquo;s a chance she will become part of the basketball team at her college. She goes on to explain that she is \"quick\" and \"tough.\" Based on these characteristics, she thinks she has a chance to join the team. Thus, the main purpose of the text is to explain why the narrator thinks she might make the basketball team.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text focuses solely on basketball and doesn&rsquo;t mention any other kinds of sports. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe aspects of the game of basketball. Instead, it provides the narrator&rsquo;s reasoning for thinking that she might make her school basketball team. Choice C is incorrect. Although the narrator explains why she thinks she will be chosen for the basketball team at her school, the text doesn&rsquo;t go into the general decision-making process or the requirements for being picked for the team.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323036,"updateDate":1730147323036}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323036, 1730147323036),
    ($SATQ$d5ad34f0$SATQ$, $SATQ$6dedae33-5cfb-49d1-bc61-2ec133b77a3d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The results of randomized clinical trials testing the efficacy of common medical interventions sometimes fail to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> conclusions that practitioners reach based on their real-world observations of patients. While there are several possible reasons for this, one is that practitioners may overlook confounding variables that account for the results they attribute to the interventions in question.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0e2dcb69-085a-4b0d-a22f-4fdf15d88e23","content":"<p>circumvent</p>"},{"id":"876fcd61-6258-4d7d-bf78-c895a9eb2116","content":"<p>corroborate</p>"},{"id":"aa7df731-50e6-4e44-b147-7b7de8687ce8","content":"<p>disseminate</p>"},{"id":"f8950581-2a11-427a-bb88-cafaed66c006","content":"<p>implement</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["876fcd61-6258-4d7d-bf78-c895a9eb2116"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of a relationship between the results of randomized clinical tests of how effective common medical interventions are and the conclusions practitioners reach about such interventions in real-world settings. In this context, "corroborate" means confirm or support with evidence. The text indicates that one possible explanation for the relationship being discussed is that practitioners may overlook confounding variables&mdash;that is, additional factors other than the medical interventions being investigated that affect the observed outcomes. This means that practitioners may assume that an outcome is the direct result of a medical intervention when it is actually the result of a combination of factors. Clinical trials take steps to rule out factors other than the one being studied, so if those extra factors are actually having an effect on real-world outcomes, the trials are likely to produce conclusions different from those practitioners reach in their real-world observations. In other words, clinical trials may fail to corroborate practitioners&rsquo; conclusions.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the results of clinical trials could "circumvent," or find a way around or bypass, conclusions practitioners reach in real-world scenarios with patients; it&rsquo;s possible that researchers conducting the trials might avoid engaging with practitioners&rsquo; conclusions, but findings from a study can&rsquo;t choose to get around something. Choice C is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the results of clinical trials could "disseminate," or spread widely, conclusions practitioners reach in real-world scenarios with patients; the researchers conducting the trials might choose to draw attention to practitioners&rsquo; conclusions, but findings from a study can&rsquo;t spread anything. Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the results of clinical trials could "implement," or put into effect, conclusions practitioners reach in real-world scenarios with patients; the researchers conducting the trials might consider practitioners&rsquo; conclusions, but findings from a study can&rsquo;t put anything into effect.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d5ad34f0","external_id":"6dedae33-5cfb-49d1-bc61-2ec133b77a3d","vaultid":null,"uId":"0cdb9b9f-c640-40ee-b187-d9166f095120","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The results of randomized clinical trials testing the efficacy of common medical interventions sometimes fail to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> conclusions that practitioners reach based on their real-world observations of patients. While there are several possible reasons for this, one is that practitioners may overlook confounding variables that account for the results they attribute to the interventions in question.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0e2dcb69-085a-4b0d-a22f-4fdf15d88e23","content":"<p>circumvent</p>"},{"id":"876fcd61-6258-4d7d-bf78-c895a9eb2116","content":"<p>corroborate</p>"},{"id":"aa7df731-50e6-4e44-b147-7b7de8687ce8","content":"<p>disseminate</p>"},{"id":"f8950581-2a11-427a-bb88-cafaed66c006","content":"<p>implement</p>"}],"keys":["876fcd61-6258-4d7d-bf78-c895a9eb2116"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of a relationship between the results of randomized clinical tests of how effective common medical interventions are and the conclusions practitioners reach about such interventions in real-world settings. In this context, \"corroborate\" means confirm or support with evidence. The text indicates that one possible explanation for the relationship being discussed is that practitioners may overlook confounding variables&mdash;that is, additional factors other than the medical interventions being investigated that affect the observed outcomes. This means that practitioners may assume that an outcome is the direct result of a medical intervention when it is actually the result of a combination of factors. Clinical trials take steps to rule out factors other than the one being studied, so if those extra factors are actually having an effect on real-world outcomes, the trials are likely to produce conclusions different from those practitioners reach in their real-world observations. In other words, clinical trials may fail to corroborate practitioners&rsquo; conclusions.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the results of clinical trials could \"circumvent,\" or find a way around or bypass, conclusions practitioners reach in real-world scenarios with patients; it&rsquo;s possible that researchers conducting the trials might avoid engaging with practitioners&rsquo; conclusions, but findings from a study can&rsquo;t choose to get around something. Choice C is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the results of clinical trials could \"disseminate,\" or spread widely, conclusions practitioners reach in real-world scenarios with patients; the researchers conducting the trials might choose to draw attention to practitioners&rsquo; conclusions, but findings from a study can&rsquo;t spread anything. Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the results of clinical trials could \"implement,\" or put into effect, conclusions practitioners reach in real-world scenarios with patients; the researchers conducting the trials might consider practitioners&rsquo; conclusions, but findings from a study can&rsquo;t put anything into effect.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669208,"updateDate":1743430669208}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669208, 1743430669208),
    ($SATQ$d69bc408$SATQ$, $SATQ$2c3579f5-5a54-410e-8872-288a52fb95e3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4ff6180c-31ad-42d1-9945-8fdc43dcb3ca$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Aphra Behn&rsquo;s 1689 novel <em>The Lucky Mistake</em>. Atlante and Rinaldo are neighbors who have been secretly exchanging letters through Charlot, Atlante&rsquo;s sister.</p>
<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>[Atlante] gave this letter to Charlot; who immediately ran into the balcony with it, where she still found Rinaldo in a melancholy posture, leaning his head on his hand: She showed him the letter, but was afraid to toss it to him, for fear it might fall to the ground; so he ran and fetched a long cane, which he cleft at one end, and held it while she put the letter into the cleft, and stayed not to hear what he said to it. But never was man so transported with joy, as he was at the reading of this letter; it gives him new wounds; for to the generous, nothing obliges love so much as love.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"00c6f113-d30c-43c6-b9df-03955c985bdf","content":"<p>It describes the delivery of a letter, and then portrays a character&rsquo;s happiness at reading that letter.</p>"},{"id":"5293a71c-965b-48e4-a892-c10a40c754c3","content":"<p>It establishes that a character is desperate to receive a letter, and then explains why another character has not yet written that letter.</p>"},{"id":"009b4d72-9675-47b8-a5d3-06cd94c9bb15","content":"<p>It presents a character&rsquo;s concerns about delivering a letter, and then details the contents of that letter.</p>"},{"id":"f668701b-4a64-4c43-aeba-1517f0e579ad","content":"<p>It reveals the inspiration behind a character&rsquo;s letter, and then emphasizes the excitement that another character feels upon receiving that letter.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["00c6f113-d30c-43c6-b9df-03955c985bdf"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is&nbsp;the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The narrator begins by explaining how Charlot carefully delivers Atlante&rsquo;s letter to Rinaldo, and then relates that Rinaldo feels &ldquo;transported with joy&rdquo; after reading the letter. Therefore, the overall structure of the text is best described as a description of the delivery of a letter followed by the portrayal of a character&rsquo;s happiness after reading the letter. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that the letter has been written; there&rsquo;s no explanation why another character hasn&rsquo;t written one. In addition, the text&rsquo;s description of Rinaldo &ldquo;in a melancholy posture&rdquo; suggests that he&rsquo;s sad and thoughtful, not that he&rsquo;s desperate to receive the letter. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text states that Charlot won&rsquo;t toss the letter to Rinaldo because she doesn&rsquo;t want it to fall, the text doesn&rsquo;t refer to the contents of the letter. Instead, the text describes how happy Rinaldo feels after reading it.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although the text does describe Rinaldo&rsquo;s reaction to the letter, the text doesn&rsquo;t begin by discussing Atlante&rsquo;s inspiration for writing the letter. Instead, the text begins by discussing the delivery of the letter.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-031-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$abff8dde-04a2-4929-8f5a-9ecffb38b50b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d69bc408","external_id":"2c3579f5-5a54-410e-8872-288a52fb95e3","vaultid":"4ff6180c-31ad-42d1-9945-8fdc43dcb3ca","uId":"cea5f5ab-4538-45a7-9529-dde6612b574b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Aphra Behn&rsquo;s 1689 novel <em>The Lucky Mistake</em>. Atlante and Rinaldo are neighbors who have been secretly exchanging letters through Charlot, Atlante&rsquo;s sister.</p>\n<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>[Atlante] gave this letter to Charlot; who immediately ran into the balcony with it, where she still found Rinaldo in a melancholy posture, leaning his head on his hand: She showed him the letter, but was afraid to toss it to him, for fear it might fall to the ground; so he ran and fetched a long cane, which he cleft at one end, and held it while she put the letter into the cleft, and stayed not to hear what he said to it. But never was man so transported with joy, as he was at the reading of this letter; it gives him new wounds; for to the generous, nothing obliges love so much as love.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"00c6f113-d30c-43c6-b9df-03955c985bdf","content":"<p>It describes the delivery of a letter, and then portrays a character&rsquo;s happiness at reading that letter.</p>"},{"id":"5293a71c-965b-48e4-a892-c10a40c754c3","content":"<p>It establishes that a character is desperate to receive a letter, and then explains why another character has not yet written that letter.</p>"},{"id":"009b4d72-9675-47b8-a5d3-06cd94c9bb15","content":"<p>It presents a character&rsquo;s concerns about delivering a letter, and then details the contents of that letter.</p>"},{"id":"f668701b-4a64-4c43-aeba-1517f0e579ad","content":"<p>It reveals the inspiration behind a character&rsquo;s letter, and then emphasizes the excitement that another character feels upon receiving that letter.</p>"}],"keys":["00c6f113-d30c-43c6-b9df-03955c985bdf"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is&nbsp;the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The narrator begins by explaining how Charlot carefully delivers Atlante&rsquo;s letter to Rinaldo, and then relates that Rinaldo feels &ldquo;transported with joy&rdquo; after reading the letter. Therefore, the overall structure of the text is best described as a description of the delivery of a letter followed by the portrayal of a character&rsquo;s happiness after reading the letter. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that the letter has been written; there&rsquo;s no explanation why another character hasn&rsquo;t written one. In addition, the text&rsquo;s description of Rinaldo &ldquo;in a melancholy posture&rdquo; suggests that he&rsquo;s sad and thoughtful, not that he&rsquo;s desperate to receive the letter. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text states that Charlot won&rsquo;t toss the letter to Rinaldo because she doesn&rsquo;t want it to fall, the text doesn&rsquo;t refer to the contents of the letter. Instead, the text describes how happy Rinaldo feels after reading it.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although the text does describe Rinaldo&rsquo;s reaction to the letter, the text doesn&rsquo;t begin by discussing Atlante&rsquo;s inspiration for writing the letter. Instead, the text begins by discussing the delivery of the letter.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-031-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"abff8dde-04a2-4929-8f5a-9ecffb38b50b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959319,"updateDate":1691007959319}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959319, 1691007959319),
    ($SATQ$d6c77ae5$SATQ$, $SATQ$2b57698d-356d-421f-be23-218f93c7aa09$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$955b6b95-66ab-43e9-938c-c0a29ded6cfb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Astronomer Mark Holland and colleagues examined four white dwarfs&mdash;small, dense remnants of past stars&mdash;in order to determine the composition of exoplanets that used to orbit those stars. Studying wavelengths of light in the white dwarf atmospheres, the team reported that traces of elements such as lithium and sodium support the presence of exoplanets with continental crusts similar to Earth&rsquo;s.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Past studies of white dwarf atmospheres have concluded that certain exoplanets had continental crusts. Geologist Keith Putirka and astronomer Siyi Xu argue that those studies unduly emphasize atmospheric traces of lithium and other individual elements as signifiers of the types of rock found on Earth. The studies don&rsquo;t adequately account for different minerals made up of various ratios of those elements, and the possibility of rock types not found on Earth that contain those minerals.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would Putirka and Xu (Text 2) most likely characterize the conclusion presented in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f9c1b570-1bb5-43f9-89b8-534c7356fd54","content":"<p>As unexpected, because it was widely believed at the time that white dwarf exoplanets lack continental crusts</p>"},{"id":"809fab2f-c44b-4813-bd15-c0be15dd4c09","content":"<p>As premature, because researchers have only just begun trying to determine what kinds of crusts white dwarf exoplanets had</p>"},{"id":"95f180e2-a3e3-4338-af09-494de92e6a0f","content":"<p>As questionable, because it rests on an incomplete consideration of potential sources of the elements detected in white dwarf atmospheres</p>"},{"id":"d66e4a2e-4811-41bb-916c-64646c594368","content":"<p>As puzzling, because it&rsquo;s unusual to successfully detect lithium and sodium when analyzing wavelengths of light in white dwarf atmospheres</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["95f180e2-a3e3-4338-af09-494de92e6a0f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it reflects how Putirka and Xu (Text 2) would likely characterize the conclusion presented in Text 1. Text 1 discusses a study by Mark Holland and colleagues in which they detected traces of lithium and sodium in the atmospheres of four white dwarf stars. The team claims that this supports the idea that exoplanets with continental crusts like Earth&rsquo;s once orbited these stars. Text 2 introduces Putirka and Xu, who indicate that sodium and lithium are present in several different minerals and that some of those minerals might exist in types of rock that are not found on Earth. Therefore, Putirka and Xu would likely describe the conclusion in Text 1 as questionable because it does not consider that lithium and sodium are also found in rocks that are not like Earth&rsquo;s continental crust. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the texts do not indicate how widely held any of the viewpoints described are.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because neither text discusses how new this area of study is. Choice D is incorrect because neither text discusses how likely lithium and sodium are to be detected by analyzing wavelengths of light.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-005-CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$8362028d-a140-4169-bb7f-8b7b62b14581$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d6c77ae5","external_id":"2b57698d-356d-421f-be23-218f93c7aa09","vaultid":"955b6b95-66ab-43e9-938c-c0a29ded6cfb","uId":"31b3859e-ebcc-479c-9a56-a19e7b3b228f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Astronomer Mark Holland and colleagues examined four white dwarfs&mdash;small, dense remnants of past stars&mdash;in order to determine the composition of exoplanets that used to orbit those stars. Studying wavelengths of light in the white dwarf atmospheres, the team reported that traces of elements such as lithium and sodium support the presence of exoplanets with continental crusts similar to Earth&rsquo;s.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>Past studies of white dwarf atmospheres have concluded that certain exoplanets had continental crusts. Geologist Keith Putirka and astronomer Siyi Xu argue that those studies unduly emphasize atmospheric traces of lithium and other individual elements as signifiers of the types of rock found on Earth. The studies don&rsquo;t adequately account for different minerals made up of various ratios of those elements, and the possibility of rock types not found on Earth that contain those minerals.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would Putirka and Xu (Text 2) most likely characterize the conclusion presented in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f9c1b570-1bb5-43f9-89b8-534c7356fd54","content":"<p>As unexpected, because it was widely believed at the time that white dwarf exoplanets lack continental crusts</p>"},{"id":"809fab2f-c44b-4813-bd15-c0be15dd4c09","content":"<p>As premature, because researchers have only just begun trying to determine what kinds of crusts white dwarf exoplanets had</p>"},{"id":"95f180e2-a3e3-4338-af09-494de92e6a0f","content":"<p>As questionable, because it rests on an incomplete consideration of potential sources of the elements detected in white dwarf atmospheres</p>"},{"id":"d66e4a2e-4811-41bb-916c-64646c594368","content":"<p>As puzzling, because it&rsquo;s unusual to successfully detect lithium and sodium when analyzing wavelengths of light in white dwarf atmospheres</p>"}],"keys":["95f180e2-a3e3-4338-af09-494de92e6a0f"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it reflects how Putirka and Xu (Text 2) would likely characterize the conclusion presented in Text 1. Text 1 discusses a study by Mark Holland and colleagues in which they detected traces of lithium and sodium in the atmospheres of four white dwarf stars. The team claims that this supports the idea that exoplanets with continental crusts like Earth&rsquo;s once orbited these stars. Text 2 introduces Putirka and Xu, who indicate that sodium and lithium are present in several different minerals and that some of those minerals might exist in types of rock that are not found on Earth. Therefore, Putirka and Xu would likely describe the conclusion in Text 1 as questionable because it does not consider that lithium and sodium are also found in rocks that are not like Earth&rsquo;s continental crust. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the texts do not indicate how widely held any of the viewpoints described are.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because neither text discusses how new this area of study is. Choice D is incorrect because neither text discusses how likely lithium and sodium are to be detected by analyzing wavelengths of light.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-005-CTC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"8362028d-a140-4169-bb7f-8b7b62b14581","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959312,"updateDate":1691007959312}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959312, 1691007959312),
    ($SATQ$d72b325e$SATQ$, $SATQ$d53760bb-41c2-4232-a2eb-abb0d8e0b051$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$453672fe-bd8a-4029-b43b-e42b9e6d0243$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Text 1</p>
<p>What factors influence the abundance of species in a given ecological community? Some theorists have argued that historical diversity is a major driver of how diverse an ecological community eventually becomes: differences in community diversity across otherwise similar habitats, in this view, are strongly affected by the number of species living in those habitats at earlier times.&nbsp;</p>
<p>Text 2</p>
<p>In 2010, a group of researchers including biologist Carla C&aacute;ceres created artificial pools in a New York forest. They stocked some pools with a diverse mix of zooplankton species and others with a single zooplankton species and allowed the pool communities to develop naturally thereafter. Over the course of four years, C&aacute;ceres and colleagues periodically measured the species diversity of the pools, finding&mdash;contrary to their expectations&mdash;that by the end of the study there was little to no difference in the pools&rsquo; species diversity.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would C&aacute;ceres and colleagues (Text 2) most likely describe the view of the theorists presented in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"27ae8d8c-4f21-488c-b1fc-f93bfd25996e","content":"<p>It is largely correct, but it requires a minor refinement in light of the research team&rsquo;s results.</p>"},{"id":"27fa796c-41c3-4a9e-b79c-f1a23b623700","content":"<p>It is not compelling as a theory regardless of any experimental data collected by the research team.</p>"},{"id":"4e679000-2fd4-43da-9ef1-2e7317fe1717","content":"<p>It may seem plausible, but it is not supported by the research team&rsquo;s findings.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2bef5707-752e-4832-8e86-0b3948d3ec68","content":"<p>It probably holds true only in conditions like those in the research team&rsquo;s study.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4e679000-2fd4-43da-9ef1-2e7317fe1717"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. This is how C&aacute;ceres and co. would most likely describe the view presented in Text 1. The view in Text 1 is that historical diversity affects how diverse an ecological community eventually becomes. But C&aacute;ceres and co. did not get this result: they found no difference in eventual diversity between a zooplankton pool that started out diverse and a zooplankton pool that started out with only a single species.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. C&aacute;ceres and co. would probably not describe the view presented in Text 1 this way. The view in Text 1 is that historical diversity affects how diverse an ecological community eventually becomes. C&aacute;ceres and co&rsquo;s findings directly undermine this view: they found no difference in eventual diversity between a zooplankton pool that started out diverse and a zooplankton pool that started out with only a single species. Choice B is incorrect. C&aacute;ceres and co. would probably not describe the view presented in Text 1 this way. Their experiment was designed to test this hypothesis, and their findings were "contrary to their expectations." In other words, before the study, they predicted the theory was correct. Choice D is incorrect. C&aacute;ceres and co. would not describe the view presented in Text 1 this way. Their research finding directly undermines the view presented in Text 1: so it definitely doesn&rsquo;t hold true in conditions like those in the study.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-001-POV$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$20384ad7-2f68-4a1b-82d4-3e30924caae1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d72b325e","external_id":"d53760bb-41c2-4232-a2eb-abb0d8e0b051","vaultid":"453672fe-bd8a-4029-b43b-e42b9e6d0243","uId":"433feab2-72d6-49dc-880f-84b0c0387318","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Text 1</p>\n<p>What factors influence the abundance of species in a given ecological community? Some theorists have argued that historical diversity is a major driver of how diverse an ecological community eventually becomes: differences in community diversity across otherwise similar habitats, in this view, are strongly affected by the number of species living in those habitats at earlier times.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Text 2</p>\n<p>In 2010, a group of researchers including biologist Carla C&aacute;ceres created artificial pools in a New York forest. They stocked some pools with a diverse mix of zooplankton species and others with a single zooplankton species and allowed the pool communities to develop naturally thereafter. Over the course of four years, C&aacute;ceres and colleagues periodically measured the species diversity of the pools, finding&mdash;contrary to their expectations&mdash;that by the end of the study there was little to no difference in the pools&rsquo; species diversity.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would C&aacute;ceres and colleagues (Text 2) most likely describe the view of the theorists presented in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"27ae8d8c-4f21-488c-b1fc-f93bfd25996e","content":"<p>It is largely correct, but it requires a minor refinement in light of the research team&rsquo;s results.</p>"},{"id":"27fa796c-41c3-4a9e-b79c-f1a23b623700","content":"<p>It is not compelling as a theory regardless of any experimental data collected by the research team.</p>"},{"id":"4e679000-2fd4-43da-9ef1-2e7317fe1717","content":"<p>It may seem plausible, but it is not supported by the research team&rsquo;s findings.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2bef5707-752e-4832-8e86-0b3948d3ec68","content":"<p>It probably holds true only in conditions like those in the research team&rsquo;s study.</p>"}],"keys":["4e679000-2fd4-43da-9ef1-2e7317fe1717"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. This is how C&aacute;ceres and co. would most likely describe the view presented in Text 1. The view in Text 1 is that historical diversity affects how diverse an ecological community eventually becomes. But C&aacute;ceres and co. did not get this result: they found no difference in eventual diversity between a zooplankton pool that started out diverse and a zooplankton pool that started out with only a single species.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. C&aacute;ceres and co. would probably not describe the view presented in Text 1 this way. The view in Text 1 is that historical diversity affects how diverse an ecological community eventually becomes. C&aacute;ceres and co&rsquo;s findings directly undermine this view: they found no difference in eventual diversity between a zooplankton pool that started out diverse and a zooplankton pool that started out with only a single species. Choice B is incorrect. C&aacute;ceres and co. would probably not describe the view presented in Text 1 this way. Their experiment was designed to test this hypothesis, and their findings were \"contrary to their expectations.\" In other words, before the study, they predicted the theory was correct. Choice D is incorrect. C&aacute;ceres and co. would not describe the view presented in Text 1 this way. Their research finding directly undermines the view presented in Text 1: so it definitely doesn&rsquo;t hold true in conditions like those in the study.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-001-POV","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"20384ad7-2f68-4a1b-82d4-3e30924caae1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959311,"updateDate":1691007959311}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959311, 1691007959311),
    ($SATQ$d74788e1$SATQ$, $SATQ$1432b988-4f53-4926-a8ee-9523e1af1e79$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>British painter Peter Edwards has a <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> painting portraits of notable figures from a variety of different fields. These characteristic works include his esteemed portraits of poet Wendy Cope and soccer player and coach Ryan Griggs.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d6150c75-145d-4b4e-ba8a-007630c61079","content":"<p>complaint about</p>"},{"id":"6b4c2899-a8a4-424d-9611-07dbdfcf76fb","content":"<p>reputation for</p>"},{"id":"973ac512-9f55-452a-b633-64221a7e5a65","content":"<p>question about</p>"},{"id":"2a2fd244-6014-4716-86d2-8f38efd6dc2c","content":"<p>requirement for</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6b4c2899-a8a4-424d-9611-07dbdfcf76fb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Peter Edwards as a portrait painter. In this context, having a "reputation for" something means that one is widely recognized for being associated with a particular activity. The text indicates that Edwards has created "characteristic works" including "esteemed portraits" of notable figures, suggesting he is known and respected for this type of painting. This choice clearly indicates that Edwards has established recognition or fame specifically in connection with painting portraits of well-known individuals.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text presents Edwards&rsquo;s portrait painting in a positive manner, describing his works as "esteemed." In this context, "complaint about" would mean dissatisfaction or grievance regarding the activity of painting portraits, which contradicts the positive characterization of Edwards&rsquo;s work. Nothing in the text suggests that Edwards has expressed any dissatisfaction with or objection to painting portraits of notable figures. Choice C is incorrect because the text gives no indication that Edwards has a "question about" painting portraits, which in this context would mean uncertainty or doubt regarding this activity. Instead, the text presents Edwards as someone who has already created multiple portrait works described as "characteristic" and "esteemed," suggesting established practice rather than uncertainty. The mention of specific examples of his portrait work further demonstrates that he is not questioning his portrait painting. Choice D is incorrect because the text does not suggest that Edwards has a "requirement for" painting portraits, which in this context would mean a mandatory obligation or necessary condition. Instead, the text indicates that portrait painting is something Edwards is known for doing well, not something he is obligated to do. The text describes his portrait work as "characteristic," suggesting it&rsquo;s a defining feature of his artistic practice rather than an externally imposed requirement.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d74788e1","external_id":"1432b988-4f53-4926-a8ee-9523e1af1e79","vaultid":null,"uId":"cd118b67-b3c9-418e-9b03-bb47a5b8668c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>British painter Peter Edwards has a <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> painting portraits of notable figures from a variety of different fields. These characteristic works include his esteemed portraits of poet Wendy Cope and soccer player and coach Ryan Griggs.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d6150c75-145d-4b4e-ba8a-007630c61079","content":"<p>complaint about</p>"},{"id":"6b4c2899-a8a4-424d-9611-07dbdfcf76fb","content":"<p>reputation for</p>"},{"id":"973ac512-9f55-452a-b633-64221a7e5a65","content":"<p>question about</p>"},{"id":"2a2fd244-6014-4716-86d2-8f38efd6dc2c","content":"<p>requirement for</p>"}],"keys":["6b4c2899-a8a4-424d-9611-07dbdfcf76fb"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Peter Edwards as a portrait painter. In this context, having a \"reputation for\" something means that one is widely recognized for being associated with a particular activity. The text indicates that Edwards has created \"characteristic works\" including \"esteemed portraits\" of notable figures, suggesting he is known and respected for this type of painting. This choice clearly indicates that Edwards has established recognition or fame specifically in connection with painting portraits of well-known individuals.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text presents Edwards&rsquo;s portrait painting in a positive manner, describing his works as \"esteemed.\" In this context, \"complaint about\" would mean dissatisfaction or grievance regarding the activity of painting portraits, which contradicts the positive characterization of Edwards&rsquo;s work. Nothing in the text suggests that Edwards has expressed any dissatisfaction with or objection to painting portraits of notable figures. Choice C is incorrect because the text gives no indication that Edwards has a \"question about\" painting portraits, which in this context would mean uncertainty or doubt regarding this activity. Instead, the text presents Edwards as someone who has already created multiple portrait works described as \"characteristic\" and \"esteemed,\" suggesting established practice rather than uncertainty. The mention of specific examples of his portrait work further demonstrates that he is not questioning his portrait painting. Choice D is incorrect because the text does not suggest that Edwards has a \"requirement for\" painting portraits, which in this context would mean a mandatory obligation or necessary condition. Instead, the text indicates that portrait painting is something Edwards is known for doing well, not something he is obligated to do. The text describes his portrait work as \"characteristic,\" suggesting it&rsquo;s a defining feature of his artistic practice rather than an externally imposed requirement.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175),
    ($SATQ$d7807ec8$SATQ$, $SATQ$ea9ee8af-fa30-4b37-9240-d7dc342b2d6f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Political blogs with conspicuous ideological alignments became an integral component of US media in the early 2000s. While some commentators lauded this development, asserting that such blogs had a welcome transparency missing from traditional news, less <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> observers countered that such blogs tended to ideological extremes that exacerbated political polarization to problematic levels.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"dd393ce1-4b51-467f-9dad-4396d2fd6272","content":"<p>sanguine</p>"},{"id":"28ee91a5-2566-4806-a6de-5debdc85d3d8","content":"<p>recalcitrant</p>"},{"id":"d76fb1e7-585a-4308-8f93-352c9c81297a","content":"<p>misanthropic</p>"},{"id":"4979e197-385a-4781-8e00-3c50747bd466","content":"<p>earnest</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dd393ce1-4b51-467f-9dad-4396d2fd6272"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of political blogs. In this context, "sanguine" means optimistic. The text begins by noting the rise of political blogs with readily identifiable ideological alignments in the early 2000s. The text then indicates that some commentators saw this as a positive development, citing a reason why (their difference from traditional news). Finally, the text goes on to contrast those commentators with others who have a negative opinion of the rise of political blogs (because they increase political polarization among their readers). This context supports the idea that the second group of commentators is less positive than the first: thus, the second group of commentators is less optimistic, or sanguine.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it would not make sense in this context to describe those commentators who have a negative opinion of political blogs as less "recalcitrant," or obstinately uncooperative, than those commentators who supported political blogs. Choice C is incorrect because the text gives no indication that those commentators who have a negative opinion of political blogs are less "misanthropic," or less contemptuous of humankind, than those commentators who have a positive opinion of political blogs&mdash;there is no indication in the text that those commentators who like political blogs would be contemptuous of humankind at all. Choice D is incorrect because there is no evidence that those commentators who have a negative opinion of political blogs are less "earnest," or sincere, than those who have a positive opinion of such blogs&mdash;presumably, both groups of commentators hold their beliefs with equal conviction.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d7807ec8","external_id":"ea9ee8af-fa30-4b37-9240-d7dc342b2d6f","vaultid":null,"uId":"d4a9fdfa-8157-4dc0-8003-e6d9b7c00352","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Political blogs with conspicuous ideological alignments became an integral component of US media in the early 2000s. While some commentators lauded this development, asserting that such blogs had a welcome transparency missing from traditional news, less <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> observers countered that such blogs tended to ideological extremes that exacerbated political polarization to problematic levels.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"dd393ce1-4b51-467f-9dad-4396d2fd6272","content":"<p>sanguine</p>"},{"id":"28ee91a5-2566-4806-a6de-5debdc85d3d8","content":"<p>recalcitrant</p>"},{"id":"d76fb1e7-585a-4308-8f93-352c9c81297a","content":"<p>misanthropic</p>"},{"id":"4979e197-385a-4781-8e00-3c50747bd466","content":"<p>earnest</p>"}],"keys":["dd393ce1-4b51-467f-9dad-4396d2fd6272"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of political blogs. In this context, \"sanguine\" means optimistic. The text begins by noting the rise of political blogs with readily identifiable ideological alignments in the early 2000s. The text then indicates that some commentators saw this as a positive development, citing a reason why (their difference from traditional news). Finally, the text goes on to contrast those commentators with others who have a negative opinion of the rise of political blogs (because they increase political polarization among their readers). This context supports the idea that the second group of commentators is less positive than the first: thus, the second group of commentators is less optimistic, or sanguine.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it would not make sense in this context to describe those commentators who have a negative opinion of political blogs as less \"recalcitrant,\" or obstinately uncooperative, than those commentators who supported political blogs. Choice C is incorrect because the text gives no indication that those commentators who have a negative opinion of political blogs are less \"misanthropic,\" or less contemptuous of humankind, than those commentators who have a positive opinion of political blogs&mdash;there is no indication in the text that those commentators who like political blogs would be contemptuous of humankind at all. Choice D is incorrect because there is no evidence that those commentators who have a negative opinion of political blogs are less \"earnest,\" or sincere, than those who have a positive opinion of such blogs&mdash;presumably, both groups of commentators hold their beliefs with equal conviction.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669214,"updateDate":1743430669214}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669214, 1743430669214),
    ($SATQ$d7dccee7$SATQ$, $SATQ$ad5acd03-7c25-483b-bad4-5d848fddf21d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$72dbfc0b-40c3-43d4-8c3d-47d9b6167930$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In a 2019 study, Jeremy Gunawardena and colleagues found that the single-celled protozoan <em>Stentor roeseli</em> not only uses strategies to escape irritating stimuli but also switches strategies when one fails. This evidence of protozoans sophisticatedly &ldquo;changing their minds&rdquo; demonstrates that single-celled organisms may not be limited to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> behaviors.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"48d1d60d-9d97-4811-9458-0b4f9f484517","content":"<p>aggressive</p>"},{"id":"280eb0cb-b74f-4339-a33f-32f04d5f819a","content":"<p>rudimentary</p>"},{"id":"5cf8910a-bbca-430b-90e2-8bb41b0e66a9","content":"<p>evolving</p>"},{"id":"dc8addb9-6060-4a2d-b5ec-dbb7829d87a2","content":"<p>advantageous</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["280eb0cb-b74f-4339-a33f-32f04d5f819a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of single-celled organism behavior. As used in this context, &ldquo;rudimentary&rdquo; means basic or unsophisticated. According to the text, a study of the single-celled protozoan <em>Stentor roeseli</em> showed that the organisms can switch strategies for escaping certain stimuli, &ldquo;sophisticatedly &lsquo;changing their minds&rsquo;&rdquo; and using new strategies should other strategies fail. This context suggests that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behaviors that are basic or rudimentary, since the study showed that single-celled protozoans can respond complexly to irritating stimuli. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behavior that is &ldquo;aggressive,&rdquo; or threatening. Rather, the text suggests that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behaviors that are basic, since the study of <em>Stentor roeseli</em> showed that single-celled protozoans can respond complexly to irritating stimuli. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behavior that is &ldquo;evolving,&rdquo; or advancing. Rather, the text suggests that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behaviors that are basic, since the study of <em>Stentor roeseli</em> showed that single-celled protozoans can respond complexly to irritating stimuli. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behavior that is &ldquo;advantageous,&rdquo; or helpful. Rather, the text suggests that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behaviors that are basic, since the study of <em>Stentor roeseli</em> showed that single-celled protozoans can respond complexly to irritating stimuli. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-960-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$dc3d6de1-cc77-403f-a678-9e5efed9e12e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d7dccee7","external_id":"ad5acd03-7c25-483b-bad4-5d848fddf21d","vaultid":"72dbfc0b-40c3-43d4-8c3d-47d9b6167930","uId":"ce71526c-7867-4157-8a51-3accf30d355d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In a 2019 study, Jeremy Gunawardena and colleagues found that the single-celled protozoan <em>Stentor roeseli</em> not only uses strategies to escape irritating stimuli but also switches strategies when one fails. This evidence of protozoans sophisticatedly &ldquo;changing their minds&rdquo; demonstrates that single-celled organisms may not be limited to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> behaviors.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"48d1d60d-9d97-4811-9458-0b4f9f484517","content":"<p>aggressive</p>"},{"id":"280eb0cb-b74f-4339-a33f-32f04d5f819a","content":"<p>rudimentary</p>"},{"id":"5cf8910a-bbca-430b-90e2-8bb41b0e66a9","content":"<p>evolving</p>"},{"id":"dc8addb9-6060-4a2d-b5ec-dbb7829d87a2","content":"<p>advantageous</p>"}],"keys":["280eb0cb-b74f-4339-a33f-32f04d5f819a"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of single-celled organism behavior. As used in this context, &ldquo;rudimentary&rdquo; means basic or unsophisticated. According to the text, a study of the single-celled protozoan <em>Stentor roeseli</em> showed that the organisms can switch strategies for escaping certain stimuli, &ldquo;sophisticatedly &lsquo;changing their minds&rsquo;&rdquo; and using new strategies should other strategies fail. This context suggests that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behaviors that are basic or rudimentary, since the study showed that single-celled protozoans can respond complexly to irritating stimuli. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behavior that is &ldquo;aggressive,&rdquo; or threatening. Rather, the text suggests that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behaviors that are basic, since the study of <em>Stentor roeseli</em> showed that single-celled protozoans can respond complexly to irritating stimuli. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behavior that is &ldquo;evolving,&rdquo; or advancing. Rather, the text suggests that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behaviors that are basic, since the study of <em>Stentor roeseli</em> showed that single-celled protozoans can respond complexly to irritating stimuli. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behavior that is &ldquo;advantageous,&rdquo; or helpful. Rather, the text suggests that single-celled organisms may not be limited to behaviors that are basic, since the study of <em>Stentor roeseli</em> showed that single-celled protozoans can respond complexly to irritating stimuli. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-960-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"dc3d6de1-cc77-403f-a678-9e5efed9e12e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959357,"updateDate":1691007959357}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959357, 1691007959357),
    ($SATQ$d8d1ecaa$SATQ$, $SATQ$e257f385-1988-4616-9742-5ef7a832d1db$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2248cc96-aeba-4d36-b7d7-5b13e39088fb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Business researcher Melanie Brucks and colleagues found that remote video conference meetings may be less conducive to brainstorming than in-person meetings are. The researchers suspect that video meeting participants are focused on staring at the speaker on the screen and don&rsquo;t allow their eyes or mind to wander as much, which may ultimately <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> creativity.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"680f704c-7dac-45f3-a6ed-740433acf507","content":"<p>recommend</p>"},{"id":"b8c84287-bff8-492c-8c5e-1e629a72e9e6","content":"<p>criticize</p>"},{"id":"4ed6d586-e220-4664-acad-ad1878fc585c","content":"<p>impede</p>"},{"id":"4cf72afa-f85e-4609-aa20-a6b5c6128da0","content":"<p>construct</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4ed6d586-e220-4664-acad-ad1878fc585c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The first sentence tells us that video meetings are &ldquo;less conducive to&rdquo; (meaning less good for) brainstorming. This suggests that the video meeting participants&rsquo; focus is bad for their creativity. &ldquo;Impede&rdquo; means &ldquo;delay&rdquo; or &ldquo;prevent,&rdquo; which works perfectly in this context. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice is too positive to fit the context. The first sentence tells us that video meetings are &ldquo;less conducive to&rdquo; (meaning less good for) brainstorming. This suggests that the video meeting participants&rsquo; focus is bad for their creativity. Choice B is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t make sense. The participants&rsquo; intense focus on the screen is the subject of the missing verb. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that their over-focusing &ldquo;criticizes&rdquo; their creativity. Choice D is incorrect. &ldquo;Construct&rdquo; means &ldquo;build&rdquo; or &ldquo;make,&rdquo; which is too positive to fit the context. The first sentence tells us that video meetings are &ldquo;less conducive to&rdquo; (meaning less good for) brainstorming. This suggests that the video meeting participants&rsquo; focus is bad for their creativity. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-M003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c3f45aab-519d-4ab6-8dd0-c8710c20e911$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d8d1ecaa","external_id":"e257f385-1988-4616-9742-5ef7a832d1db","vaultid":"2248cc96-aeba-4d36-b7d7-5b13e39088fb","uId":"bf877ca0-8586-4110-aac3-07798ad7356f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Business researcher Melanie Brucks and colleagues found that remote video conference meetings may be less conducive to brainstorming than in-person meetings are. The researchers suspect that video meeting participants are focused on staring at the speaker on the screen and don&rsquo;t allow their eyes or mind to wander as much, which may ultimately <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> creativity.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"680f704c-7dac-45f3-a6ed-740433acf507","content":"<p>recommend</p>"},{"id":"b8c84287-bff8-492c-8c5e-1e629a72e9e6","content":"<p>criticize</p>"},{"id":"4ed6d586-e220-4664-acad-ad1878fc585c","content":"<p>impede</p>"},{"id":"4cf72afa-f85e-4609-aa20-a6b5c6128da0","content":"<p>construct</p>"}],"keys":["4ed6d586-e220-4664-acad-ad1878fc585c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The first sentence tells us that video meetings are &ldquo;less conducive to&rdquo; (meaning less good for) brainstorming. This suggests that the video meeting participants&rsquo; focus is bad for their creativity. &ldquo;Impede&rdquo; means &ldquo;delay&rdquo; or &ldquo;prevent,&rdquo; which works perfectly in this context. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice is too positive to fit the context. The first sentence tells us that video meetings are &ldquo;less conducive to&rdquo; (meaning less good for) brainstorming. This suggests that the video meeting participants&rsquo; focus is bad for their creativity. Choice B is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t make sense. The participants&rsquo; intense focus on the screen is the subject of the missing verb. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that their over-focusing &ldquo;criticizes&rdquo; their creativity. Choice D is incorrect. &ldquo;Construct&rdquo; means &ldquo;build&rdquo; or &ldquo;make,&rdquo; which is too positive to fit the context. The first sentence tells us that video meetings are &ldquo;less conducive to&rdquo; (meaning less good for) brainstorming. This suggests that the video meeting participants&rsquo; focus is bad for their creativity. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-M003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c3f45aab-519d-4ab6-8dd0-c8710c20e911","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959378,"updateDate":1691007959378}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959378, 1691007959378),
    ($SATQ$d8d33cc5$SATQ$, $SATQ$16ffff21-d68d-458f-b3ba-778145a5ffcd$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The early British postal system required the cost of mail delivery to be paid upon receipt, a system which encouraged inventive strategies by the intended recipient to avoid payment. To improve this system, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> were proposed in 1837, including the use of a postage stamp, a small receipt pasted to the mail indicating that delivery costs had been paid by the sender.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d76bb8ef-19f5-4f81-9636-37af3a742f24","content":"<p>investigations</p>"},{"id":"6a5935cb-d9b0-45ab-ad42-fbe2e7e505e1","content":"<p>expansions</p>"},{"id":"e0649a32-44ef-4ec7-899c-dd269e84f51e","content":"<p>reforms</p>"},{"id":"629e0b05-2469-476c-bf6b-b0f7274068e4","content":"<p>possessions</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e0649a32-44ef-4ec7-899c-dd269e84f51e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the early British postal system. In this context, "reforms" most nearly means attempts at improving something. The text indicates that the pay-upon-delivery postage method led to fee avoidance on the part of recipients. It then says that action was taken to improve the postal system, giving examples such as the move to having the sender pay and affixing stamps to indicate payment had been received. These represent revisions to the system intended to improve its functioning; therefore, "reforms" most logically completes the text.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, "investigations" most nearly means attempts to understand something. And although it is plausible that an attempt to improve the postal system would involve investigations, nothing in the text provides a logical basis for understanding the example of changes like affixing stamps to letters as an investigation. Choice B is incorrect. In this context, "expansions" most nearly means additions to something. Although the text indicates that some new policies were adopted, nothing in the text suggests that the overall number of policies increased, which expansions would seem to entail. Choice D is incorrect. In this context, "possessions" most nearly means items of personal property or things that otherwise belong to, or are part of, something. The text provides no logical context for understanding changes like affixing stamps to letters as possessions.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d8d33cc5","external_id":"16ffff21-d68d-458f-b3ba-778145a5ffcd","vaultid":null,"uId":"ae3e6bf2-6af1-489a-9e87-f62179a319bf","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The early British postal system required the cost of mail delivery to be paid upon receipt, a system which encouraged inventive strategies by the intended recipient to avoid payment. To improve this system, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> were proposed in 1837, including the use of a postage stamp, a small receipt pasted to the mail indicating that delivery costs had been paid by the sender.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d76bb8ef-19f5-4f81-9636-37af3a742f24","content":"<p>investigations</p>"},{"id":"6a5935cb-d9b0-45ab-ad42-fbe2e7e505e1","content":"<p>expansions</p>"},{"id":"e0649a32-44ef-4ec7-899c-dd269e84f51e","content":"<p>reforms</p>"},{"id":"629e0b05-2469-476c-bf6b-b0f7274068e4","content":"<p>possessions</p>"}],"keys":["e0649a32-44ef-4ec7-899c-dd269e84f51e"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the early British postal system. In this context, \"reforms\" most nearly means attempts at improving something. The text indicates that the pay-upon-delivery postage method led to fee avoidance on the part of recipients. It then says that action was taken to improve the postal system, giving examples such as the move to having the sender pay and affixing stamps to indicate payment had been received. These represent revisions to the system intended to improve its functioning; therefore, \"reforms\" most logically completes the text.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, \"investigations\" most nearly means attempts to understand something. And although it is plausible that an attempt to improve the postal system would involve investigations, nothing in the text provides a logical basis for understanding the example of changes like affixing stamps to letters as an investigation. Choice B is incorrect. In this context, \"expansions\" most nearly means additions to something. Although the text indicates that some new policies were adopted, nothing in the text suggests that the overall number of policies increased, which expansions would seem to entail. Choice D is incorrect. In this context, \"possessions\" most nearly means items of personal property or things that otherwise belong to, or are part of, something. The text provides no logical context for understanding changes like affixing stamps to letters as possessions.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323041,"updateDate":1730147323041}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323041, 1730147323041),
    ($SATQ$d91e5499$SATQ$, $SATQ$40a6e0a3-6546-4f86-94a3-674e8b7539c4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A team of paleontologists has found a rich fossil deposit near Gulgong, Australia. The fossils are so well preserved that the team has been able to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> detailed information about the life forms that left them behind, such as color patterns and how they interacted with other species.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0e39fb11-60e2-4bd0-bf81-4a7687e7aa62","content":"<p>occupy</p>"},{"id":"73c2ccce-ad0f-4426-ab78-2f87c02c4b5a","content":"<p>hoard</p>"},{"id":"7034b769-f9a9-4546-89f3-cc4471082ce6","content":"<p>reserve</p>"},{"id":"d64bd3d1-ccb9-46c3-8cdd-831245b7c7ba","content":"<p>obtain</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d64bd3d1-ccb9-46c3-8cdd-831245b7c7ba"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the fossil deposit. In this context, "obtain" means gain or acquire. According to the text, a team of paleontologists has found fossils that are very well preserved. For this reason, the text suggests, the paleontologists have been able to gain detailed information from the fossils, such as the color patterns of the life forms that left them behind.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "occupy" means engage or inhabit, neither of which would make sense in context. It&rsquo;s unclear what it would mean for detailed information revealed by fossils to be engaged or inhabited. Choice B is incorrect because the text gives no indication that the paleontologists wanted to "hoard," or collect and hide, the detailed information revealed by the well-preserved fossils. Choice C is incorrect because the text gives no indication that the paleontologists wanted to "reserve," or withhold, the detailed information revealed by the well-preserved fossils.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d91e5499","external_id":"40a6e0a3-6546-4f86-94a3-674e8b7539c4","vaultid":null,"uId":"3e5d8632-19d4-4b8a-89f3-e5a2c46519f0","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A team of paleontologists has found a rich fossil deposit near Gulgong, Australia. The fossils are so well preserved that the team has been able to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> detailed information about the life forms that left them behind, such as color patterns and how they interacted with other species.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0e39fb11-60e2-4bd0-bf81-4a7687e7aa62","content":"<p>occupy</p>"},{"id":"73c2ccce-ad0f-4426-ab78-2f87c02c4b5a","content":"<p>hoard</p>"},{"id":"7034b769-f9a9-4546-89f3-cc4471082ce6","content":"<p>reserve</p>"},{"id":"d64bd3d1-ccb9-46c3-8cdd-831245b7c7ba","content":"<p>obtain</p>"}],"keys":["d64bd3d1-ccb9-46c3-8cdd-831245b7c7ba"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the fossil deposit. In this context, \"obtain\" means gain or acquire. According to the text, a team of paleontologists has found fossils that are very well preserved. For this reason, the text suggests, the paleontologists have been able to gain detailed information from the fossils, such as the color patterns of the life forms that left them behind.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"occupy\" means engage or inhabit, neither of which would make sense in context. It&rsquo;s unclear what it would mean for detailed information revealed by fossils to be engaged or inhabited. Choice B is incorrect because the text gives no indication that the paleontologists wanted to \"hoard,\" or collect and hide, the detailed information revealed by the well-preserved fossils. Choice C is incorrect because the text gives no indication that the paleontologists wanted to \"reserve,\" or withhold, the detailed information revealed by the well-preserved fossils.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323032,"updateDate":1730147323032}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323032, 1730147323032),
    ($SATQ$d9915c15$SATQ$, $SATQ$6eedf83d-8dfc-4a34-8f06-be2096a743ae$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2020, rap artist and professor A.D. Carson published the first peer-reviewed rap album about his experiences with Black masculinity called &ldquo;i used to love to dream.&rdquo; Typically in peer review, experts evaluate scholarly articles prior to publication. For Carson&rsquo;s album, dubbed a &ldquo;mixtap/e/ssay,&rdquo; peer review involved both scholars and rap artists. In combining elements of a mixtape album with scholarly essays that connect Carson&rsquo;s lyrics to historical and contemporary contexts for listeners both inside and outside academia, Carson&rsquo;s album helped redefine how scholarship is created and shared.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4e5b4b7d-d55f-4f40-817a-36c75b1c6857","content":"<p>To compare the relative public impact of scholarly articles and albums</p>"},{"id":"738f268b-6fb5-44de-975a-076cb79c9152","content":"<p>To capture one scholar&rsquo;s opinion of a new rap album</p>"},{"id":"32340fed-8fe6-4f56-898b-ff092a20c176","content":"<p>To explain why a certain rap album is particularly innovative</p>"},{"id":"0acf4c74-1782-4cbd-bfdc-e238f4be0b3b","content":"<p>To describe how each step of the peer review process unfolds</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["32340fed-8fe6-4f56-898b-ff092a20c176"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes the main purpose of the text, which is to explain why a certain rap album is particularly innovative. The text mentions rap artist and professor A.D. Carson, who published "the first peer-reviewed rap album." The text also describes the unusual process of having the work evaluated by both scholars and rap artists before its release, and details how the album combines elements of a mixtape album with elements of scholarly essays. All of these characteristics of the album and the way in which it was developed help to demonstrate the album&rsquo;s novelty.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because though the text mentions an album that combines elements of scholarly essays and mixtapes, it does not compare the relative public impact of scholarly articles and albums. Choice B is incorrect because the text does not present the opinion of a scholar regarding the rap album. Choice D is incorrect because though the text mentions that the album was peer reviewed, it does not detail the steps of the review.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d9915c15","external_id":"6eedf83d-8dfc-4a34-8f06-be2096a743ae","vaultid":null,"uId":"0a8d9bf8-77a2-4f8f-99fb-5b40b66c5e7f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2020, rap artist and professor A.D. Carson published the first peer-reviewed rap album about his experiences with Black masculinity called &ldquo;i used to love to dream.&rdquo; Typically in peer review, experts evaluate scholarly articles prior to publication. For Carson&rsquo;s album, dubbed a &ldquo;mixtap/e/ssay,&rdquo; peer review involved both scholars and rap artists. In combining elements of a mixtape album with scholarly essays that connect Carson&rsquo;s lyrics to historical and contemporary contexts for listeners both inside and outside academia, Carson&rsquo;s album helped redefine how scholarship is created and shared.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4e5b4b7d-d55f-4f40-817a-36c75b1c6857","content":"<p>To compare the relative public impact of scholarly articles and albums</p>"},{"id":"738f268b-6fb5-44de-975a-076cb79c9152","content":"<p>To capture one scholar&rsquo;s opinion of a new rap album</p>"},{"id":"32340fed-8fe6-4f56-898b-ff092a20c176","content":"<p>To explain why a certain rap album is particularly innovative</p>"},{"id":"0acf4c74-1782-4cbd-bfdc-e238f4be0b3b","content":"<p>To describe how each step of the peer review process unfolds</p>"}],"keys":["32340fed-8fe6-4f56-898b-ff092a20c176"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes the main purpose of the text, which is to explain why a certain rap album is particularly innovative. The text mentions rap artist and professor A.D. Carson, who published \"the first peer-reviewed rap album.\" The text also describes the unusual process of having the work evaluated by both scholars and rap artists before its release, and details how the album combines elements of a mixtape album with elements of scholarly essays. All of these characteristics of the album and the way in which it was developed help to demonstrate the album&rsquo;s novelty.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because though the text mentions an album that combines elements of scholarly essays and mixtapes, it does not compare the relative public impact of scholarly articles and albums. Choice B is incorrect because the text does not present the opinion of a scholar regarding the rap album. Choice D is incorrect because though the text mentions that the album was peer reviewed, it does not detail the steps of the review.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323042,"updateDate":1730147323042}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323042, 1730147323042),
    ($SATQ$d9e55268$SATQ$, $SATQ$8349ae46-df73-4387-9c4e-704a441d362e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Louise Erdrich&rsquo;s 2020 novel <em>The Night Watchman</em>. Louis Pipestone is collecting signatures for a petition from fellow members of the Turtle Mountain Band of Chippewa on the tribe&rsquo;s reservation in North Dakota.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Louis Pipestone tended the petition like a garden. He kept it with him at all times. In town, his eyes sharpened when he noticed a tribal member who hadn&rsquo;t yet signed. Wherever they were&mdash;at the gas pump, mercantile [general store], at Henry&rsquo;s [Caf&eacute;], on the road, or outside the clinic and hospital&mdash;Louis cornered them. If they were waiting for a baby to be born, he&rsquo;d have them sign. If they were laughing, if they were arguing. If they were taking a child home from school, they signed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p style="text-align: right;"><span class="dap_copyright">&copy;2020 by Louise Erdrich</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"142bebcd-7dfc-4b53-a49e-671599351c38","content":"<p>To suggest that some tribal members refuse to sign the petition because they dislike Louis Pipestone</p>"},{"id":"9fe54e5b-1df0-4877-9ccf-c15b7851cb16","content":"<p>To show that attitudes toward the petition within the tribal community change over time</p>"},{"id":"d1db0285-e054-4687-9b97-b389b9bb6015","content":"<p>To demonstrate that most tribal members are enthusiastic about signing the petition</p>"},{"id":"9d629080-3248-4e41-9040-a242a72cdff2","content":"<p>To portray Louis Pipestone&rsquo;s strong commitment to collecting signatures for the petition</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9d629080-3248-4e41-9040-a242a72cdff2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the text&rsquo;s main purpose. The text indicates that Louis always had his petition with him, asked everyone he encountered to sign it if they hadn&rsquo;t already, and lists several comical circumstances in which he might try to get someone to sign. Thus, the main purpose of the text is to illustrate Louis&rsquo;s dedicated focus on getting people to sign the petition.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text suggests that Louis was aggressive in seeking signatures for the petition&mdash;for example, saying that he "cornered" people&mdash;nothing in the text addresses how those people feel toward Louis, let alone that they are refusing his request. Choice B is incorrect because, other than portraying Louis&rsquo;s commitment to gathering signatures, the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss community members&rsquo; attitudes toward Louis or the petition. Choice C is incorrect because the text never mentions the attitudes of the people Louis approaches toward the petition, but the text does indicate that he "cornered" prospective signatories, strongly suggesting that their enthusiasm was neither needed nor considered.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d9e55268","external_id":"8349ae46-df73-4387-9c4e-704a441d362e","vaultid":null,"uId":"c486f251-594d-4d6e-afb0-a61589b83081","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Louise Erdrich&rsquo;s 2020 novel <em>The Night Watchman</em>. Louis Pipestone is collecting signatures for a petition from fellow members of the Turtle Mountain Band of Chippewa on the tribe&rsquo;s reservation in North Dakota.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Louis Pipestone tended the petition like a garden. He kept it with him at all times. In town, his eyes sharpened when he noticed a tribal member who hadn&rsquo;t yet signed. Wherever they were&mdash;at the gas pump, mercantile [general store], at Henry&rsquo;s [Caf&eacute;], on the road, or outside the clinic and hospital&mdash;Louis cornered them. If they were waiting for a baby to be born, he&rsquo;d have them sign. If they were laughing, if they were arguing. If they were taking a child home from school, they signed.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><span class=\"dap_copyright\">&copy;2020 by Louise Erdrich</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"142bebcd-7dfc-4b53-a49e-671599351c38","content":"<p>To suggest that some tribal members refuse to sign the petition because they dislike Louis Pipestone</p>"},{"id":"9fe54e5b-1df0-4877-9ccf-c15b7851cb16","content":"<p>To show that attitudes toward the petition within the tribal community change over time</p>"},{"id":"d1db0285-e054-4687-9b97-b389b9bb6015","content":"<p>To demonstrate that most tribal members are enthusiastic about signing the petition</p>"},{"id":"9d629080-3248-4e41-9040-a242a72cdff2","content":"<p>To portray Louis Pipestone&rsquo;s strong commitment to collecting signatures for the petition</p>"}],"keys":["9d629080-3248-4e41-9040-a242a72cdff2"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the text&rsquo;s main purpose. The text indicates that Louis always had his petition with him, asked everyone he encountered to sign it if they hadn&rsquo;t already, and lists several comical circumstances in which he might try to get someone to sign. Thus, the main purpose of the text is to illustrate Louis&rsquo;s dedicated focus on getting people to sign the petition.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text suggests that Louis was aggressive in seeking signatures for the petition&mdash;for example, saying that he \"cornered\" people&mdash;nothing in the text addresses how those people feel toward Louis, let alone that they are refusing his request. Choice B is incorrect because, other than portraying Louis&rsquo;s commitment to gathering signatures, the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss community members&rsquo; attitudes toward Louis or the petition. Choice C is incorrect because the text never mentions the attitudes of the people Louis approaches toward the petition, but the text does indicate that he \"cornered\" prospective signatories, strongly suggesting that their enthusiasm was neither needed nor considered.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323041,"updateDate":1730147323041}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323041, 1730147323041),
    ($SATQ$daaed806$SATQ$, $SATQ$0ff467f3-58be-4ab7-9e76-c8c0d4ba2ee2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$283dc769-8e1e-4701-a1af-f059bcf02afd$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>M&ocirc;nica Lopes-Ferreira and others at Brazil&rsquo;s Butantan Institute are studying the freshwater stingray species <em>Potamotrygon rex</em> to determine whether biological characteristics such as the rays&rsquo; age and sex have <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> effect on the toxicity of their venom&mdash;that is, to see if differences in these traits are associated with considerable variations in venom potency.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b46f27ec-e313-49ab-963a-eb40f9c47f3f","content":"<p>a disconcerting</p>"},{"id":"98d2ffd3-3818-4851-a72b-1a620829155e","content":"<p>an acceptable</p>"},{"id":"f126b01d-bfa3-455c-b670-5cb49db58f6b","content":"<p>an imperceptible</p>"},{"id":"6076cc27-371c-46c2-ae50-9d9f40a3fab6","content":"<p>a substantial</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6076cc27-371c-46c2-ae50-9d9f40a3fab6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the research that Lopes-Ferreira and her colleagues are conducting on the stingray species <em>Potamotrygon rex.</em> As used in this context, &ldquo;a substantial&rdquo; effect means an effect that is sizable or noteworthy. The text indicates that the researchers are seeking to determine whether there are &ldquo;considerable variations&rdquo; in the potency of stingray venom that are associated with variation in the stingrays&rsquo; age and sex. This context suggests that the researchers want to find out whether stingray age and sex have a substantial effect on venom toxicity. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in the text that suggests that the researchers have been studying whether the stingrays&rsquo; age and sex have &ldquo;a disconcerting,&rdquo; or an unsettling and disturbing, effect on the stingrays&rsquo; venom. The text indicates that the researchers wish to determine if stingray age and sex cause large variations in the toxicity of stingray venom, not if the effect of age and sex is disconcerting. Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that researchers want to find out whether differences in stingray age and sex produce differences in stingray venom, not that the researchers want to find out whether age and sex have &ldquo;an acceptable,&rdquo; or a satisfactory, effect on venom. The text makes no mention of what would make an effect on venom toxicity acceptable and gives no indication that the researchers are interested in that question. Choice C is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context for the researchers to be looking for &ldquo;an imperceptible,&rdquo; or an unnoticeable, effect of age and sex on stingray venom. The text says that the researchers are trying to determine if there are &ldquo;considerable variations&rdquo; in venom toxicity linked to age and sex, not that the researchers are trying to find effects that they can&rsquo;t perceive. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-933-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e4eddd9a-aa54-4515-8865-54becbe5b6d1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"daaed806","external_id":"0ff467f3-58be-4ab7-9e76-c8c0d4ba2ee2","vaultid":"283dc769-8e1e-4701-a1af-f059bcf02afd","uId":"5851a5b0-7613-4b3d-bd93-81340589d130","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>M&ocirc;nica Lopes-Ferreira and others at Brazil&rsquo;s Butantan Institute are studying the freshwater stingray species <em>Potamotrygon rex</em> to determine whether biological characteristics such as the rays&rsquo; age and sex have <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> effect on the toxicity of their venom&mdash;that is, to see if differences in these traits are associated with considerable variations in venom potency.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b46f27ec-e313-49ab-963a-eb40f9c47f3f","content":"<p>a disconcerting</p>"},{"id":"98d2ffd3-3818-4851-a72b-1a620829155e","content":"<p>an acceptable</p>"},{"id":"f126b01d-bfa3-455c-b670-5cb49db58f6b","content":"<p>an imperceptible</p>"},{"id":"6076cc27-371c-46c2-ae50-9d9f40a3fab6","content":"<p>a substantial</p>"}],"keys":["6076cc27-371c-46c2-ae50-9d9f40a3fab6"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the research that Lopes-Ferreira and her colleagues are conducting on the stingray species <em>Potamotrygon rex.</em> As used in this context, &ldquo;a substantial&rdquo; effect means an effect that is sizable or noteworthy. The text indicates that the researchers are seeking to determine whether there are &ldquo;considerable variations&rdquo; in the potency of stingray venom that are associated with variation in the stingrays&rsquo; age and sex. This context suggests that the researchers want to find out whether stingray age and sex have a substantial effect on venom toxicity. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in the text that suggests that the researchers have been studying whether the stingrays&rsquo; age and sex have &ldquo;a disconcerting,&rdquo; or an unsettling and disturbing, effect on the stingrays&rsquo; venom. The text indicates that the researchers wish to determine if stingray age and sex cause large variations in the toxicity of stingray venom, not if the effect of age and sex is disconcerting. Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that researchers want to find out whether differences in stingray age and sex produce differences in stingray venom, not that the researchers want to find out whether age and sex have &ldquo;an acceptable,&rdquo; or a satisfactory, effect on venom. The text makes no mention of what would make an effect on venom toxicity acceptable and gives no indication that the researchers are interested in that question. Choice C is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context for the researchers to be looking for &ldquo;an imperceptible,&rdquo; or an unnoticeable, effect of age and sex on stingray venom. The text says that the researchers are trying to determine if there are &ldquo;considerable variations&rdquo; in venom toxicity linked to age and sex, not that the researchers are trying to find effects that they can&rsquo;t perceive. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-933-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e4eddd9a-aa54-4515-8865-54becbe5b6d1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959356,"updateDate":1691007959356}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959356, 1691007959356),
    ($SATQ$dba9eaf8$SATQ$, $SATQ$76babb0f-bee5-4ab9-8e63-133a4f711925$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$511b4b72-eb8d-4bdb-8c3e-baee9316893c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Within baleen whale species, some individuals develop an accessory spleen&mdash;a seemingly functionless formation of splenetic tissue outside the normal spleen. Given the formation&rsquo;s greater prevalence among whales known to make deeper dives, some researchers hypothesize that its role isn&rsquo;t <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>; rather, the accessory spleen may actively support diving mechanisms.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"68bc6f7c-d508-4b3b-827b-7042acdfefcb","content":"<p>replicable</p>"},{"id":"ac9571ed-94af-4a56-9b14-fc83f58c1c74","content":"<p>predetermined</p>"},{"id":"e924ca97-7e6e-4337-936e-810e7b48f05f","content":"<p>operative</p>"},{"id":"fecc3bb7-b6fe-44b9-a198-634aedbe2217","content":"<p>latent</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["fecc3bb7-b6fe-44b9-a198-634aedbe2217"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of baleen whale accessory spleens. In this context, &ldquo;latent&rdquo; means dormant or functionless. The text sets up a contrast between the idea that baleen whale accessory spleens appear not to have a function and the research indicating that the accessory spleen may actually have a role in supporting the whales&rsquo; diving mechanisms. This context therefore conveys the idea that the assumption that baleen whale accessory spleens are latent may be incorrect. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the role of the accessory spleen is &ldquo;replicable,&rdquo; or capable of being reproduced. The text indicates that the role of the accessory spleen seems to have no function, but some researchers think it does have a role; the text doesn&rsquo;t address whether the role of the accessory spleen could or couldn&rsquo;t be reproduced. Choice B is incorrect because suggesting that the role of the accessory spleen is &ldquo;predetermined,&rdquo; or decided in advance, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. Although the researchers may agree that the role of the accessory spleen or any other organ hasn&rsquo;t been determined in advance, the text focuses on the idea that the accessory spleen was thought to have been functionless but may in fact serve an active role for baleen whales. Choice C is incorrect because it&rsquo;s the opposite of what the context of the text is conveying. The second sentence of the text indicates that baleen whale accessory spleens may not be useless, not that they aren&rsquo;t &ldquo;operative,&rdquo; or functional. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-950-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$06edb90c-d7e2-4e12-bb3a-fe56db52d132$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dba9eaf8","external_id":"76babb0f-bee5-4ab9-8e63-133a4f711925","vaultid":"511b4b72-eb8d-4bdb-8c3e-baee9316893c","uId":"f1df2b80-1bf0-4f62-b29e-cca73e9a0ec8","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Within baleen whale species, some individuals develop an accessory spleen&mdash;a seemingly functionless formation of splenetic tissue outside the normal spleen. Given the formation&rsquo;s greater prevalence among whales known to make deeper dives, some researchers hypothesize that its role isn&rsquo;t <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>; rather, the accessory spleen may actively support diving mechanisms.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"68bc6f7c-d508-4b3b-827b-7042acdfefcb","content":"<p>replicable</p>"},{"id":"ac9571ed-94af-4a56-9b14-fc83f58c1c74","content":"<p>predetermined</p>"},{"id":"e924ca97-7e6e-4337-936e-810e7b48f05f","content":"<p>operative</p>"},{"id":"fecc3bb7-b6fe-44b9-a198-634aedbe2217","content":"<p>latent</p>"}],"keys":["fecc3bb7-b6fe-44b9-a198-634aedbe2217"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of baleen whale accessory spleens. In this context, &ldquo;latent&rdquo; means dormant or functionless. The text sets up a contrast between the idea that baleen whale accessory spleens appear not to have a function and the research indicating that the accessory spleen may actually have a role in supporting the whales&rsquo; diving mechanisms. This context therefore conveys the idea that the assumption that baleen whale accessory spleens are latent may be incorrect. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the role of the accessory spleen is &ldquo;replicable,&rdquo; or capable of being reproduced. The text indicates that the role of the accessory spleen seems to have no function, but some researchers think it does have a role; the text doesn&rsquo;t address whether the role of the accessory spleen could or couldn&rsquo;t be reproduced. Choice B is incorrect because suggesting that the role of the accessory spleen is &ldquo;predetermined,&rdquo; or decided in advance, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. Although the researchers may agree that the role of the accessory spleen or any other organ hasn&rsquo;t been determined in advance, the text focuses on the idea that the accessory spleen was thought to have been functionless but may in fact serve an active role for baleen whales. Choice C is incorrect because it&rsquo;s the opposite of what the context of the text is conveying. The second sentence of the text indicates that baleen whale accessory spleens may not be useless, not that they aren&rsquo;t &ldquo;operative,&rdquo; or functional. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-950-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"06edb90c-d7e2-4e12-bb3a-fe56db52d132","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959356,"updateDate":1691007959356}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959356, 1691007959356),
    ($SATQ$dc043599$SATQ$, $SATQ$7e3611fc-9f46-405f-94ba-467e4976e0e1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$dd626c53-4c14-42d1-8b83-2fe4051b4b47$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p>
<p>Most scientists agree that the moon was likely formed after a collision between Earth and a large planet named Theia. This collision likely created a huge debris field, made up of material from both Earth and Theia. Based on models of this event, scientists believe that the moon was formed from this debris over the course of thousands of years.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Text 2</strong>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Researchers from NASA&rsquo;s Ames Research Center used a computer to model how the moon could have formed. Although simulations of the moon&rsquo;s formation have been done in the past, the team from NASA ran simulations that were much more detailed. They found that the formation of the moon was likely not a slow process that took many years. Instead, it&rsquo;s probable that the moon&rsquo;s formation happened immediately after impact, taking just a few hours.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes a difference in how the author of Text 1 and the author of Text 2 view the evidence for the formation of the moon?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"99ba42c7-a144-49f0-bb3b-94c02d95c1d2","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 argues that the formation of the moon occurred much earlier than the author of Text 2 argues.</p>"},{"id":"34e6fb9f-01e3-4f40-8b3c-f7fe1f53a36e","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 suggests there is more evidence confirming the existence of Theia than the author of Text 2 suggests.</p>"},{"id":"a5b6444e-b842-4b9b-a1d9-fb765d5adb8c","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 claims that the moon&rsquo;s surface is more similar to Earth&rsquo;s surface than the author of Text 2 claims.</p>"},{"id":"16cff228-9e6b-4af3-815d-bcd8363e2e58","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 believes that the moon formed more slowly than the author of Text 2 believes.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["16cff228-9e6b-4af3-815d-bcd8363e2e58"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. Text 1 states that the moon was formed from the debris &ldquo;over the course of thousands of years,&rdquo; while Text 2 states that the moon&rsquo;s formation happened &ldquo;immediately after impact, taking just a few hours.&rdquo; This shows a clear difference in how the authors view the evidence for the speed of the moon&rsquo;s formation. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While Text 2 suggests that the moon formed over &ldquo;just a few hours&rdquo; and Text 1 says it took &ldquo;thousands of years,&rdquo; neither one mentions when that formation occurred. Choice B is incorrect. While Theia isn&rsquo;t mentioned in Text 2, neither text describes or disputes evidence of Theia&rsquo;s existence. Choice C is incorrect. Neither text makes any claims about the similarity or difference between the moon&rsquo;s surface and Earth&rsquo;s surface. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-E009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4ca3ae7a-6416-4844-9e57-01409f17d3b8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dc043599","external_id":"7e3611fc-9f46-405f-94ba-467e4976e0e1","vaultid":"dd626c53-4c14-42d1-8b83-2fe4051b4b47","uId":"88e6365c-d4ce-4511-8d25-62899115824c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p>\n<p>Most scientists agree that the moon was likely formed after a collision between Earth and a large planet named Theia. This collision likely created a huge debris field, made up of material from both Earth and Theia. Based on models of this event, scientists believe that the moon was formed from this debris over the course of thousands of years.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Text 2</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Researchers from NASA&rsquo;s Ames Research Center used a computer to model how the moon could have formed. Although simulations of the moon&rsquo;s formation have been done in the past, the team from NASA ran simulations that were much more detailed. They found that the formation of the moon was likely not a slow process that took many years. Instead, it&rsquo;s probable that the moon&rsquo;s formation happened immediately after impact, taking just a few hours.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes a difference in how the author of Text 1 and the author of Text 2 view the evidence for the formation of the moon?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"99ba42c7-a144-49f0-bb3b-94c02d95c1d2","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 argues that the formation of the moon occurred much earlier than the author of Text 2 argues.</p>"},{"id":"34e6fb9f-01e3-4f40-8b3c-f7fe1f53a36e","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 suggests there is more evidence confirming the existence of Theia than the author of Text 2 suggests.</p>"},{"id":"a5b6444e-b842-4b9b-a1d9-fb765d5adb8c","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 claims that the moon&rsquo;s surface is more similar to Earth&rsquo;s surface than the author of Text 2 claims.</p>"},{"id":"16cff228-9e6b-4af3-815d-bcd8363e2e58","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 believes that the moon formed more slowly than the author of Text 2 believes.</p>"}],"keys":["16cff228-9e6b-4af3-815d-bcd8363e2e58"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. Text 1 states that the moon was formed from the debris &ldquo;over the course of thousands of years,&rdquo; while Text 2 states that the moon&rsquo;s formation happened &ldquo;immediately after impact, taking just a few hours.&rdquo; This shows a clear difference in how the authors view the evidence for the speed of the moon&rsquo;s formation. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While Text 2 suggests that the moon formed over &ldquo;just a few hours&rdquo; and Text 1 says it took &ldquo;thousands of years,&rdquo; neither one mentions when that formation occurred. Choice B is incorrect. While Theia isn&rsquo;t mentioned in Text 2, neither text describes or disputes evidence of Theia&rsquo;s existence. Choice C is incorrect. Neither text makes any claims about the similarity or difference between the moon&rsquo;s surface and Earth&rsquo;s surface. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-E009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4ca3ae7a-6416-4844-9e57-01409f17d3b8","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959361,"updateDate":1691007959361}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959361, 1691007959361),
    ($SATQ$dc0bca21$SATQ$, $SATQ$33281590-ff75-47d2-8820-e0840888f7c8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>It would be a mistake to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the exhibit that artist and curator Joe Baker, who is a member of the Lenape (Delaware) people, has organized at the Brooklyn Public Library. The exhibit, which includes Lenape beadwork from the 1850s as well as modern works that use traditional patterns, is essential viewing.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5576641f-4321-4475-b0c5-017e06f1e0c6","content":"<p>complicate</p>"},{"id":"81db8eab-90f5-4746-a161-b7d519ec4b01","content":"<p>amplify</p>"},{"id":"6b9a2af8-95df-44be-8c49-8941d0950250","content":"<p>overlook</p>"},{"id":"48983c27-df29-4825-a80e-b1cbfb6107b1","content":"<p>assemble</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6b9a2af8-95df-44be-8c49-8941d0950250"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of an exhibit at the Brooklyn Public Library. In this context, "overlook" means to ignore or to miss. The text states that artist and curator Joe Baker organized an exhibit that features Lenape beadwork in historical and modern works. The text describes the exhibit as "essential viewing," suggesting that it&rsquo;s an important exhibit that people should make an effort to visit; this context conveys that it would be a mistake to overlook the exhibit.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, "complicate" would mean to add difficulty, and there&rsquo;s no reason to think the text would need to directly caution against complicating Baker&rsquo;s exhibit; the text focuses on the idea that the exhibit is "essential viewing," not on the idea of a potential difficulty that could be raised. Choice B is incorrect. In this context, "amplify" means to increase, and there&rsquo;s no reason to believe the text would indicate that it would be a mistake to increase an exhibit the text describes as "essential viewing." Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that it would be a mistake to "assemble," or put together, Baker&rsquo;s exhibit: the text makes it clear that the exhibit has already been put together and, further, praises the exhibit as "essential viewing."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dc0bca21","external_id":"33281590-ff75-47d2-8820-e0840888f7c8","vaultid":null,"uId":"e18aabe2-36c4-4c69-a86c-702de885c438","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>It would be a mistake to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the exhibit that artist and curator Joe Baker, who is a member of the Lenape (Delaware) people, has organized at the Brooklyn Public Library. The exhibit, which includes Lenape beadwork from the 1850s as well as modern works that use traditional patterns, is essential viewing.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5576641f-4321-4475-b0c5-017e06f1e0c6","content":"<p>complicate</p>"},{"id":"81db8eab-90f5-4746-a161-b7d519ec4b01","content":"<p>amplify</p>"},{"id":"6b9a2af8-95df-44be-8c49-8941d0950250","content":"<p>overlook</p>"},{"id":"48983c27-df29-4825-a80e-b1cbfb6107b1","content":"<p>assemble</p>"}],"keys":["6b9a2af8-95df-44be-8c49-8941d0950250"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of an exhibit at the Brooklyn Public Library. In this context, \"overlook\" means to ignore or to miss. The text states that artist and curator Joe Baker organized an exhibit that features Lenape beadwork in historical and modern works. The text describes the exhibit as \"essential viewing,\" suggesting that it&rsquo;s an important exhibit that people should make an effort to visit; this context conveys that it would be a mistake to overlook the exhibit.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, \"complicate\" would mean to add difficulty, and there&rsquo;s no reason to think the text would need to directly caution against complicating Baker&rsquo;s exhibit; the text focuses on the idea that the exhibit is \"essential viewing,\" not on the idea of a potential difficulty that could be raised. Choice B is incorrect. In this context, \"amplify\" means to increase, and there&rsquo;s no reason to believe the text would indicate that it would be a mistake to increase an exhibit the text describes as \"essential viewing.\" Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that it would be a mistake to \"assemble,\" or put together, Baker&rsquo;s exhibit: the text makes it clear that the exhibit has already been put together and, further, praises the exhibit as \"essential viewing.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323034,"updateDate":1730147323034}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323034, 1730147323034)
